     One of the manuals in the OpenVMS documentation set is
 "OpenVMS Performance Management".  This manual supersedes two
 older manuals, "Guide to OpenVMS Performance Management" and
 "Guide to OpenVMS AXP Performance Management" by re-combining
 the material from those two manuals (which originally came from
 the same source).

     This manual has (in our opinion) a lot of very useful
 information on system performance management, measurement, and
 improvement, including diagnosing existing systems.

     We (some of the people within DIGITAL working on OpenVMS
 Performance and Documentation) are considering making additional
 changes and updates to this manual, but would first like to get
 some idea of what the user community thinks of it, and how it's
 being used.

     We would like to know, first of all, have any of you (or
 any of the people you work with or discuss these matters with)
 read or skimmed through any of these manuals?  Did you find it
 useful as background material?  As a reference point for
 solving problems?  As something else?  (Computer oriented,
 please, not as a door stop ;-).

     If you have used it, is there anything about it you liked,
 or disliked, or wished it had, or could be improved if it was
 reorganized in some way?  What sort of audience do you think it
 should be aimed at: or to put it another way, will it be useful
 or how can it be made useful to the people you think will be
 running OpenVMS systems in the near future?  Or does anyone
 think it's just fine the way it is?

     If you've never looked at this manual before, could you
 please consider looking at it now and giving some opinions on
 it?

     Thanks very much.

 If you prefer to respons privately you can send mail to
 the "reply to" address after removing the disabling
 section.  I apologize in advance to polite users of
 News, but the actions of a few unpricipled people and
 firms make it necessary to 'mangle' all addresses in
 news postings so they can't be 'trolled' for bulk
 e-mailing.

