bernd rieke wrote:

> There is only one way to reach the ultimative open system world:
>     when everything comes only from one company
> ... and I fear the world is going this way....

Um... I think there's a little confusion about what "open system" means.

By definition, an open system is one in which all components do _not_
come from one vendor, or at least don't have to.

Some years ago, I was a Scitex operator. Scitex was a closed system. I
had a Scitex workstation, with a Scitex interface, and a Scitex film
plotter. I ran Scitex software. If my keyboard broke, I'd have to get a
replacement _from Scitex._

Same goes for products from Quantel. Hell, Quantel didn't even have a
TCP/IP stack on their Boxes until recently.

An open system is based on standards that more than one company agrees
to. Macintoshes are an open system, sort of, because I can buy a
computer from one vendor, a monitor from another, software from another,
and so on.

Same with Windows PCs. Same with Unix workstations from Sun or HP or
SGI.

None of these is a totally open system. Software written for Sun can
sometimes be recompiled to run on SGI, but not always. Peripheral
devices for one system will often work with another, but not without
some work on some level.

If all the computer hardware and software companies in the world
vanished from the face of the earth tomorrow, leaving only Packard Bell,
we would _not_ have an "open system."

-- 
Jeff Harrell, Systems Engineer
Digital Media Performance Labs, Inc.
   http://www.dmpl.com
   http://jeff.dmpl.com



