I had to revise my comparisons after Shane Hebert pointed out
a rather bizarre result -- when he recompiled the code with
"blend" rather "Pentium" code generation (under Solaris), there
was a big speed jump for the PPro.  I checked, and the results
are correct; somehow picking a 486/Pentium "blend" was almost
1.5 times as efficient as pure Pentium code! I tried the same trick with
VC++, and got a commensurate speed jump... so it is looking
murkier in terms of the relative efficiencies...
Shane also got the the 512k L2 PPro down to 430 seconds...

Old prologue:
"Below are some recent timings for a "Lattice Boltzmann" code
I use for calculating fluid flow and chemical transport (see
http://www.sandia.gov/eesector/gs/gc/movies.htm
for examples of usage).  The "benchmark" was one of our
working codes, stripped of the graphics and GUI; the
algorithms have fairly high locality of reference, and use
about 11 MB of dynamically allocated arrays."

New comparison:
===================================================================
CPU/MHz       Compiler                      Time(sec)  Perform/PPro
---           --------                      ---------  ------------
PPro 200    VC++,Pentium, "maximize speed"     655        0.692
            "Pentium" code generation"

USparc 200  Solaris 2.5, -fast-xO5-xchip=ultra
            -xarch=v8plusa-                    419        1.081

PPro 200    VC++,Pentium, "maximize speed"     453        1.000
            "blend" for code generation

R10K 195    SGI -O3-n32-mips4-LNO:opt=1:       413*       1.097
            prefetch=2:prefetch_ahead=3:
            ou=4,8
21164A 433  DEC C X5.6-043 -std1-O5-migrate    228        1.987
            -non_shared -tune -ev5 -fast (Unix)
21164A 500              "                      198        2.288
21164A 500  gcc **                             327        1.385

*Estimate, since the SGI owner ran a problem
that was 55 MB, rather than the requested 11 MB;
however, other tests suggest the SGI updates/(econd*site)
are about the same for >> 1 MB data.

**Based on statement that gcc was 1.65 times slower than DEC cc.

Machines:
PPro is Gateway2000 system with 256K L2, 32 MB 60 ns EDO.
USparc is UltraSparc 200 MHz with 1 MB L2 (from Shane Hebert).
SGI is Indigo 2, 195 MHz R10K, 1 MB L2 cache.
Alpha's both have 1 MB L3; they were supposed to be
the "affordable" variety (e.g. as sold by Enorex).



