  "Maria Suggitt" <maria_suggitt@spectratek.co.uk> wrote:
>
> Has anyone, anywhere had any problems running processes on Digital Unix
> 3.2G. We seem to be getting random process crashes with this version but
> identical software runs fine on 3.2C
>
> Any suggestions, hints, tips, ideas would be gratefully received !
> --
Dear Maria,

The system could be killing applications due to lack of
swap space.

There are 2 types of swap allocation policies
1. Lazy swap allocation(over-commitment/deferred allocation )
   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
   is where swap space is allocated only when it is needed.
   This method requires less swap space to support a set of
   tasks.However, with this method, a process may be killed
   asynchronously if swap space is exhausted.
2. Eager swap allocation(immediate swap allocation)
   ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
   is where swap space is reserved at task creation for the
   entire data space.This method is a swap reservation
   system. The actual swap space is allocated when a demand
   zero fault or copy-on-write operation takes place.

If the soft-link '/sbin/swapdefault' exists then you are using
the eager swap mode.
You can change to the eager mode by placing a symbolic link
to your swap partition eg.
# ln -s /dev/rz0b /sbin/swapdefault

The link should look something like this like this eg.
# ls -l /sbin/swap*
lrwxr-xr-x 1 root 10 1 1997 Mar 1997  swapdefault->../dev/rz0b

Let me know if this helps....
Regards,
Jude Cruz, TIME Systems Integrators, MALAYSIA

