Ferran Hermosa <ecotec@abaforum.es> wrote in article
<5f3iue$ik8$1@diana.ibernet.es>...
> 
> Im having trouble trying to upgrade a 486/66 CPU to a Intel PODP5V83
> Overdrive Microprocessor. The system works fine with old processor but
> when I change the chip sometimes hangs, dont'boot hard /floppy disk,
> work few minutes...etc.
> 
> The system is a Digital XL Server 4/66 with 128Kb cache, NT Server
> 3.51 and last Bios update.
> 
> Dec telephonic support think that souldn't change any jumper.
> 
> Anybody have the magic answer? Thanks

It is correct, that no jumpers should be changed, if you replace
a 486DX2 (66MHz) with a 83MHz Pentium OverDrive for a 486 socket.

However, I have a recollection that for this particular machine
you must have BIOS rev 1.23 *AND* you must disable the L2 (external)
cache in CMOS SETUP (which negates much of the benefit of the
Pentium OverDrive processor). 

I don't know if things have changed (like that there's a BIOS release 
that doesn't require disabling of the L2 cache).

The page at

    http://www.pc.digital.com/~ftp/index/ix_DECpc_XL.htm

still lists 1.23 as the latest BIOS release for the machine.

Anyway, if using the above BIOS and disabling the L2 cache
makes the hangups go away, then you have a partial solution.

Digital has (or used to have) also "PowerGrade" upgrades with
which you could upgrade to a newer architecture (essentially
it includes the Celebris XL motherboard, CPU daughtercard and
CPU + the work to install the stuff).

The newer motherboard is required on older DECpc XL systems
because the newer Intel Pentium chipset ("Neptune") is partly
on the CPU daughtercard (3 chips) and the motherboard (1 chip).

You need the newer chipset to support newer Pentium processors
(and the new stuff also has Plug and Play, PnP, Advanced Power
Management, APM, and Enhance IDE, EIDE), if my memory serves
me right. ;-)

The drawback is the major price (read: expensive) of the PowerGrade 
upgrades

