In article <5f24rp$3lu@nnrp1.news.primenet.com>, Jeff Shirley <slinker@primenet.com_> writes:

: (I am aware from other posts that the KA650 requires a different
: cabinet kit than a KA630).

   You may be considering physical differences in the mounting, as
   BA2xx-class enclosures usually have a bulkhead handle for the
   Q-bus modules or CPUs -- the electronics, display, wiring, etc.,
   are interchangeable between the KA630, KA650, and KA655.  There
   are BA23/BA123 and BA2xx bulkheads for both the KA630 and the
   KA650 -- a few BA2xx-based MicroVAX II KA630 systems were sold,
   and a few BA23 KA650 systems were sold.)

   I've seen some KA6xx modules with integrated S-box handles, and
   some with detachable handles.

:1) Does a KA640 really have on-board Ethernet, or am I missing something?

   Yes, the KA640 provides on-board Ethernet and DSSI connections.

:2) How do a KA640 and KA650 compare in performance?

   KA640 is slightly slower than the KA650 -- 2.4 vs 2.7 VUPS or so.
   At the time, the difference was noticable.  Now, they both seem
   very slow...

:3) Am I missing any issues?

   KA650 can be installed into the BA23, BA123, BA213 and BA215
   enclosures.  There BA23 and BA123 used the same cabinet kit, save
   for the length of the ribbon cable.  The BA213 and BA215 use the
   same s-box bulkhead "handle" for the KA650 and KA630.  The KA650
   cabinet kits and wiring are compatible with the KA630 (MicroVAX
   II) and KA655 (MicroVAX 3800) (non-module-handle-integrated) CPU
   cabinet kits, but are not compatible with the KA640 cabinet kit.

