It is impossible to predict the future with certainty, however:

In article <5evl6q$3vl$2@fastware.kiev.ua>, egor@fastware.kiev.ua (Egor Egorov) writes:
> There is a flame about OpenVMS here, I see. What's up with VMS - are DEC
> about to drop this product ?

DEC claims they are not about to drop VMS.

Many customers say they do not want DEC to drop VMS.

Financial analysts say DEC is getting a large amount of their revenue
from customers who do not want DEC to drop VMS.

> Is VMS supported ?

Yes.  While there have been efforts at DEC to reduce the variety of
different types of support contracts, these have not been so successful
as efforts to eliminate two separate part numbers for exactly the same
disk drive.

Aside from formal support, you will find people from DEC's VMS group
answering questions on comp.os.vms and on DECUServe more often than
in prior years.  Of course most technical questions which might be
answered in a newsgroup do not require someone from DEC to answer
them.  In fact, most of them are about how to get rid of a false
"You have new mail" message, even though that is fully answered
in the VMS FAQ.

> Will there be new versions ?

DEC claims to be working on new versions.

They have talked about specific features they plan to put into
new versions.

The have talked about more general features they would like to
put into (later) new versions.

None of that talk is (or has ever been) a legal commitment until
an SPD is published at the time the new version is released.

In recent years, however, DEC has done a _better_ job of getting
new versions of VMS out on the schedule they planned.

They have not necessarily done a better job of ensuring that all
subscription renewals get processed, so keep an eye on discussion
groups to keep abreast of when to expect new versions.

Larry Kilgallen



