Edward T. wrote:
> 
> On Tue, 18 Feb 1997 11:03:22 -0600, c'est moi <this@is.me> wrote:
> 
> >I heard about some place that has managed to make
> >a 8088 compatible chip running at > 2000MHz.
> 
> Obviously you should be able to clock it at 2000Mhz....
> 
> But you'd have to replace the chip every few microseconds :-)
> 
> Clear Surf,
> 
> Edward.

It might not manage the slew rates necessary to change between 0 and 1
logic levels.  As for the cooling, how about putting the chip in some
liquid coolant.  No, I don't mean the DIP, but the actual die itself.
:-)

The problem is that even if somebody had an 8088 running at 2000Mhz, the
speed may not impress you because I don't see what sort of memory could
keep up with it.  Even the fastest cache memory would be much too slow. 
Then there is the problem that you're stuck with a 1 meg address space
unless you want bank switching.  However, if you had some application
that needed only a little bit of memory, and memory that is fast enough
could be manufactured, then this could work.

This 2000Mhz 8088 stuff may be a joke, but I have read about a DSP that
runs at 1000 Mhz, so it isn't that far fetched.  The PowerPC
(Exponential's BiCMOS version) and Alpha can both do 500, so we are
certainly getting there.

