> >:floppy drive to boot, or mount the cdrom.
> >
> >Try installing a real operating system on it.
> >
> >Isildur
> >
> 
> Grow up, will you.
> 
> NT is a real operating system, as evidenced by the ever increasing
> number of businesses which are adopting it. NT may not be ready for
> Enterprise Resource Planning, but it sure is ready for file & print
> services, electronic mail systems and departmental applications.
> 
> You guys are beginning to sound like the OS/2 and Amiga victims.

Well, I can't see how NT can be considered to be anything close to Unix
or VMS.  Most of my experience is with Unix so I'll use it as an example
of why NT isn't a real OS.

First of all, NT is made by one company.  There is no standard about
it.  There are no alternatives.  There are many Unix implementations out
there, including free Unix implementations.

Now consider the administration of an NT system.  First of all, you just
have to be there, because there isn't a good standard remote access
method.  Then, you're stuck with graphical tools.  Sure, moving pictures
around may be fun for some, but when you try to automate things, you'll
probably be stuck.

Then there are all the useful Unix command line utilities like grep,
sed, awk, tr, etc.  These are incredibly useful.  On NT you're stuck
with some text editor. 
How about the fact the NT doesn't come with a compiler?  Any real OS has
got to be able to compile C code.  (Well, okay, some Unix vendors may
sell the compiler separately)
Yes, by installing GNU tools, you will fix many of these shortcomings. 
However, remember that these are *Unix* tools. 

There is also the programming question.  Compared to the elegant unix
system calls, the Win32 API is a real horror.  Just take a look at all
those functions which take many parameters, many of which are actually
structures.
Then, consider the platforms supported by Unix.  It can run on anything
from a lowly 8086 to a Cray.  Can NT do that?  What about having decent
performance on a 386DX?

Sure, NT is good enough for many things, but it isn't a real OS yet, and
knowing Microsoft, it'll probably never get there.

