Eric Dittman wrote:
> 
> In article <331215B6.40DFEB9C@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca> you wrote:
> : Well, I haven't done this yet.  I've done the reverse, that is used a PC
> : monitor for VAXstation 2000 video.  Howver, I know a web site that would
> : proably have some information that you need.  Check out the:
> 
> Boris, how did you use a PC monitor on a VS2000?

Well, the signal levels are quite comaptible with VGA with sync on
green.  You can just plug the VAXstation video output into the BNC
connectors on your monitor.  If you just have a 15 pin high density
connector, get a BNC jack and a 15 pin high density (PC monitor) female
connector and make an adapter.  (You may be able to buy an adapter
somewhere.  I don't know)
My VAXstation 2000 had just the standard monochrome video, so I just
connected:
BNC centre --- pin 2 (green)
BNC shield --- pin 7 (green ground)
If you have a color card, get 2 more BNC jacks, and remember that red,
green, blue is 1, 2, 3 with corresponding returns (grounds) at 6, 7, 8.
Oh yes, you will have to use a soldering iron or crimp tool to make
these connections.

The monitor was a MAG Innovision MX-15F, which can handle sync on
green.  The picture was very good but of course green.

There are some considerations here:

1) Your monitor *must* be able to handle 60kHz horizontal scan rate.
(that's what I measured with my oscilloscope).  Don't force your monitor
outside its specs because that may damage it.  It may be possible to do
some twisted hacks to interlace the video or something if you really,
really must use it on a cheap monitor, but the quality would probably
suck.

2)  Most PC monitors will *not* do sync on green.  You'd have to build
an adapter to extract the sync.  If the monitor can do composite sync, a
simple circuit involving one comparator or op-amp shoud be able to
extract the sync pulses which are at a lower voltage than the video.  If
the monitor can't do composite sync, you'd have to separate horizontal
and vertical sync, and you'd have to do quite a bit more.  
I was going to do this, but the cheap monitors I wanted to use won't
come close to 60kHz, and it looks like I'll be using NetBSD which
doesn't support the display.

3)  Don't try to run red green and blue from the single output of the
mono card unless you can set all but one input to high impedance mode. 
These are normally terminated with 75 ohms, and you'll overload the
video output.  If you can't use high impedance mode and want to do this,
buffer the signal.  (Not worth the trouble.  Green is good enough.)

Oh yes, I'm aware that some of this does require some electronics
knowledge.  If you need some help, ask me.

