Tobias,

  I only know the DEC C++ compiler for OpenVMS; could be the same code
base though.

Tobias Gloth (gloth@uni-muenster.de) wrote:
: I try to port a software package that already runs on Linux, Solaris,
: IRIX and AIX (everywhere both with the native C++-Compiler and with
: g++) to OSF1.
[...]
: So I had a look at the man-page which told me that the compile-option
: -define_templates could help.

On VMS, it's not "could help"; this switch is required.

:  
: * When I used "-define_templates", I get many strange error-messages
:   like "file.cc:x: error: in this statement, "someMethod" is not a
:   member of "variable". Looking at the code I thing that the compiler
:   is wrong. No other compiler ever complained in such a way, so it
:   looks like a cxx-problem to me. And I get lots of these errors.

Try to move the class definitions and declarations to a header file. The
DEC C++ compiler (at least on OpenVMS) resolves templates by generating
instantiated versions in temp files in a repository directory. To do
this, it must be able to include all of the necessary headers.

It could be a good idea to take a more thorough look at the cxx man page,
especially if it has a section on templated classes...

Hope it helps,
  Martin
