In article <5ergtp$hnf$1@nntp2.ba.best.com>, miimura@best.com writes:
> However, you must admit that by virtue of being able to make use of 
> software workarounds, it allows you the luxury of not fixing known hardware 
> bugs.  It is not a question of what the "right" way to fix it is, it's a 
> question of expediency and cost.  This works especially well with embedded 
> systems or systems where the hardware vendor has control over the software 
> at least at the OS level.

Oh, I'm not saying it's necessarily *bad* that manufacturers
fix hardware by s/w workarounds. In fact, I agree with you; it's
a fine way to make things work if the h/w bugs can be
reasonably worked around (i.e. without degradation of
quality or performance).

I posted my comments in response to someone who claimed that 
"hardware was more reliable than software," and attempted to
back this up by asking "when was the last time you encountered
a CPU bug?" The truth is that on most machines, you are hitting
small CPU bugs *all the time.* They are just worked around in the 
operating system or compilers, so you don't see them.

I still agree that h/w is in general more reliable than s/w,
but I took exception to the previous poster's line of reasoning.

	-Doug



