Steve Strange USG <strange@zk3.dec.com> wrote:

>You also have the option of using AdvFS to create multi-volume
>filesystems.  This can be more flexible if you want to enlarge or
>shrink your filesystems on-line.

Speaking of multi-disk filesystems & growing them on-line, I was
hoping DEC might have plans to incorporate a mechanism for growing
a filesystem into LSM.  The Solaris "Solstice DiskSuite" includes the
command growfs(1m) which "non-destructively expands a mounted or unmounted
file system up to the size of the file system's partition." This
capability would be very useful in combination with both UFS & ADVFS
in Digital UNIX.

[Yes, I know you can add volumes to an advfs file domain.  However, as far
as I can tell, the only way to stripe an entire fileset is to build
your advfs file domain on top of a striped LSM volume.  When doing this, you
loose an incredible amount of flexibility -- adding a 4GB volume to a
20GB LSM striped fileset now becomes an arduous task which involves choosing
between 4 rather unpleasant options:

a) nuke the volume & restore from tape  
	- this is painful.

b) assemble 20BG of spare disk & add to the advfs file domain; rmvol
   the orig LSM volume from the advfs domain; add the new disk to the LSM
   vol.; stripe it; add it to the advfs file domain; remove the 20GB of
   spare disk from your advfs file domain
	- not many shops have enough spare disk space laying around to
	  implement this solution

c) simply add the 4GB disk into the advfs file domain
	- this disk won't be striped so you won't be getting the full
	  performance out of your disk array.

d) give up & run solaris on your file servers
	- now that they finally have nfsv3, this is a real option

]

I'm quite jealous of my colleagues running DiskSuite... 

Drew


