Bob Riggs (riggs_b@biles.com) wrote:
: Can anyone help this guy, or is this just going to degenerate into an
: argument about whose OS is better?

Yes.

: >> >:I have a Digital 2100.

Congratulations!  A very nice machine from all reports.

: >> >:I am trying to install NT40 server.

I'll refrain from comment here to stop any flame wars already in progress :)

: >> >:I can't get past square one. I am strictly a win person. I can't get the
: >> >:floppy drive to boot, or mount the cdrom.

Okay, here's what you do.  First, you need to determine if you are in the 
ARC or SRM console.  These are basically the bootloaders.  If your screen 
looks like this:

>>>

you are in SRM.  Type in "set os_type nt" (without the "quotes") and do a 
hard reset (like, turn the damn thing off and back on again :)

This should bring you to the ARC console.  The ARC console looks like a 
very weak menu.  Better than the SRM in usability, but doesn't quite 
compete with TurboVision (alright, I'm an old Pascal hacker...college 
cured me of that, though).  Anyway, search through the menus.  Look for 
something that allows you to run a program.  You want to run arcinst.exe 
off the NT cdrom.  TTYTT, I forget how I did this.  I just screwed around 
for long enough.

Once you have that running, you should be able to install without muych 
problem.  A couple of caveats though:

The ARC needs to boot from a FAT partition.  Make sure C: is FAT and has 
at least 5 megs (I'm using 100 Megs so that I can move stuff between NT 
and Linux easily).  I'd definitely suggest the rest of your hard drive be 
formatted NTFS.  It's much cooler.

When choosing which disk to put it on, be careful.  The order that 
they're displayed in looks backwards.  I wrote over my Digital Unix 
install by choosing the wrong hard drive.  Bummer, but I had the disks 
and there wasn't anything important on it yet.



