Organization: Cornell University
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
X-Mailer: Mozilla 3.01 (X11; I; SunOS 4.1.4 sun4c)


For anyone that may be interested...

I previously posted that Microsoft's Powerstation Fortran was
extremely slow under FX!32.  That was before I figured out how
the optimizer worked.  Once optimized, the Fortran compiler
ran at a reasonable speed, although not blindingly fast.

I encountered one problem, though.  We tried to optimize
the compiled executable under FX!32 and while it said
'success', when we checked the log, we had the following
error:

opt: Unsuppotred floating point instruction:00002C92:FSAVE
dword ptr [EBP - 200]

opt: Processed 118 routines: translated 117 routines (1 routine
untranslatable)

The optimized code would run well to a point and then get 
slow.

So, do any of the FX!32 gurus out their have any ideas ?  I 
know this is totally unsupported and generally not a good idea.
The point for me, however, is not if it's a good idea, but
can it be done.  If it can, I'd like to figure out how.

Jeff



