Oleg Krivosheev wrote:
 
> tim@maths.tcd.ie (Timothy Murphy) writes:

> > hahn@neurocog.lrdc.pitt.edu (Mark Hahn) writes:

> > >the bottom line was that gcc produces code that's 25-30% slower
> > >than native compilers on the Alpha or P6.

> > What "native compilers" on P6?

> it is Intel Reference Compiler - what a surprise ;)

I just saw a DDJ review of commercial C compilers, including the
Intel-ref compiler used as a back-end for Borland C.
On ByteMarks, the Borland/Intel Ref did give a prett dramatic
integer performance improvement over Watcom -- about a factor
of 1.59 I believe.  And on real applications, there was (of
course) little difference among the compilers.

I just finished benchmarking Watcom, Symantec and MS VC++ on
one of our recent computational programs (another float-intensive
program).  The predictions of ByteMark were:

MS = Watcom = 2*Symantec

Reality was:

MS= 1.05*Symantec;  Synamtec= 1.3*Watcom...

In other words, ByteMark FP proved to be a miserable
predictor of floating point performance.

Now the code goes off for testing on various other
processors.  So far, a 195 MHz R10000 is <1.7 times
faster than the 200 MHz PPro.



