>             that should land you with enough wedge to buy you a
>system that a) dosent require 3phase b) will fit into your den c) will
>be still more than powerful enough to learn admin stuff on.

Actually, there is a workaround for the 3-phase requirement that will
let it run on 220V single-phase. It's described in the VAXBSD FAQ,
which can be found at www.bitcon.no/~gunnar/vaxbsd/FAQ.html (under 
section 6.5, as I recall). Since part of the workaround involves 
removing a 3-phase transformer on the main input, the overall power
consumption will go down. Even good transformers are only about 65%
efficient, so if it's normally been drawing 1.4KVA, it'd drop to 910VA,
much easier on the budget. Locally, that'd cost me about $36 per month
more in electricity--less than a decent cable TV feed, and a lot more
fun, IMHO.

Still, it's all speculative until I lay hands on it.

>Look at a Micrvax III or a VAX 4000 or maybe a VAXstation.

I may be getting a MicroVAX 3200 later this year, but it comes
with no software (again, if I get it). The 6220 has a transferable
copy of VMS on it, and if all else fails, I think it'll run VAXBSD.
But the 6220 would be local, while the 3200 is about 900 miles away.

Thanks for the response, though!

