wayne@webware-inc.com wrote:

> [...] They are trying to create a large filesystem for usenet support from multiple
> disks (2 disks right now).

> From the DEC manuals it looks like we will need to use LSM to put the disks
> into a volume.  Then it's not clear, but I think we need to mount the volume.

You initialize the LSM.  Then you create logical volumes, which causes
new block-
and character-special files to show up in /dev/vol and /dev/rvol.  These
behave like
the /dev/rzxx and /dev/rrzxx special files, so just finish up with newfs
and mount.

I have only one disk under the LSM.  In my case, it works out like this:

    # disklabel -rw /dev/rrz1c unknown  : drive spins up...
    # volsetup rz1
    # volassist -U fsgen make scratch 512m  : could be much bigger...
    # newfs -t 20 -u 109 /dev/rvol/scratch
    # fsck -no /dev/rvol/scratch
    # mkdir /usr/scratch
    # mount /dev/vol/scratch /usr/scratch

The `-t' and `-u' parameters to newfs were stolen from the disk label
created
by the first command.  Your pals will have to get their second disk
under the LSM,
but that should be documented in the on-line manual.

