Dirk Grunwald wrote:
> 
> So, this is a curious question I've been having. So why, when IA32
> emulation is done in software (as in FX!32) with some hardware assists
> for speed (byte-move, amoung other things), are people suspicious and
> the support issue is raised.
> 
> But, when IA32 emulation is done in hardware (as in P7/Merced, M2, K6,
> etc) possibly with software support, the issue just doesn't come up?
> 
> Why do people trust the software less than the hardware?
> 
> --
> Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder

Well, people in general tend to trust hardware much more than software. 
Besides Intel's Pentium division problem, hardware has been very
reliable for most people.  After all, when was the last time you
suspected that a CPU was misbehaving?  
OTOH, the reputation of software isn't even close.  This is especially
true for Microsoft software ;-)  Seriously, how many times have you
dealt with bugs in all sorts of software packages?  How about serious OS
bugs and security holes?
People have come to expect this from software.  People even put up with
vendors who just don't fix bugs.  As a matter of fact, most software
based emulators break on many applications.

There is also the performance issue.  Emulation is software is
considered "slow" and hardware is "fast".  This does have some basis. 
Many software-only emulators may only get a tenth (or even less) of the
speed of the CPU they're running on.  I've heard of this gap narrowing,
significantly in some cases, but they're almost always special cases. 
Hardware support generally removes most of the performance penalty.
Of course, you *will* pay for the extra hardware.  However, software is
often so expensive that the prices will be competitive.

