I try to port a software package that already runs on Linux, Solaris,
IRIX and AIX (everywhere both with the native C++-Compiler and with
g++) to OSF1. When I used g++, I had no trouble. However, I can't
get it to work with cxx. This is what happens:
 
* When I compile and link and everything normally (no speciel compiler
  flags), I get lots of unresolved Symbols (caused by non-instantiated
  templates). This happend with static libraries as well as with
  shared ones (I hope I built them correlty. I used the no special
  compile-flags and "ld -r -o libxxx.so file1.o file2.o ...").
 
So I had a look at the man-page which told me that the compile-option
-define_templates could help.
 
* When I used "-define_templates", I get many strange error-messages
  like "file.cc:x: error: in this statement, "someMethod" is not a
  member of "variable". Looking at the code I thing that the compiler
  is wrong. No other compiler ever complained in such a way, so it
  looks like a cxx-problem to me. And I get lots of these errors.
 
The version is (cxx -V) "DEC C++ V1.3B-0 for DEC OSF/1 (Alpha)",
running on (uname -a) "OSF1 xpi413 V3.0 358 alpha".
 
Here a comes short program that triggers the problem:
 
----
template<class T>
class Bar;
 
template<class T>
class Foo {
  friend Bar<T>;
  int dummy;
};
 
template<class T>
class Bar {
  void func();
  Foo<T>* foo;
};
 
template <class T>
void Bar<T>::func() {
  foo->dummy;
}
 
Foo<int> member;
----
$ cxx -define_templates -c bug.cc
bug.cc:18: error: In this statement, "dummy" is not a member of "foo".
Compilation terminated with errors. 
----
 
If I omit the "-define_templates", everything compiles fine...
 
    Any comments are greatly appreciated,
 
     Thanks, Tobias
 

