In article <bart.verspagen.79.0010A06A@merit.unimaas.nl>, bart.verspagen@merit.unimaas.nl writes:

>I have a small DEC 3000 Alpha Station, running OSF/1. It has a 1 Gb SCSI 
>harddisk, as well as one other SCSI device attached (an external  DAT 
>streamer). The disk is getting a bit overloaded, but I also happen to have 
>this great Seagate SCSI disk lying around doing nothing. 
>
>You probably figured it by now, my question is whether it is possible to make 
>the Seagate work in the DEC Station. The DEC's manual and OSF documentation 
>only talks about disks with DEC partnumbers. But SCSI is SCISI, isn't it?

SCSI has an ANSI Standard version, which offers a some liberty in
what might be considered a compliant device and a considerable number
of options.  Since the standard is fairly recent, there are lots of
old devices that offer features to be found in the standard, but
which were added by the vendors while the spec was still in flux.

That said, disks tend to be best behaved SCSI devices and tend to
be the easiest to add.

Also, which DEC 3000?  Which version of DEC OSF/1?  Which Seagate
disk?  As disks have gotten larger and after, they also generate
more heat.  2 GB disks tend to run hotter than 1 GB disks.  4 GB
disks hotter than 2 GB disks and 8 GB disks hotter than 4 GB.  As
of V3.2C, the RZ29B is the largest disk supported in the DEC 3000
family, so it can probably keep the 4 GB group cool.  If this
Seagate is one of the new 8 GB ones, you may want to watch the
internal temperature (if it is to be an internal device).

>If I get it installed, how do I partition the Seagate (it used to function in 
>a dos machine)?

Use the disklabel(8) command to put a label on it.  If the disk is a
well behaved SCSI device, disklabel(8) will be able to get the geometry
from the disk and a default partition table from the driver.  Early
versions will get the rotational speed wrong unless it is a 3600
rpm drive.  Once labeled you can repartition to taste and correct
any mistakes that disklabel made, if it matters.  Use disklabel for
this as well.  Once labeled, the programs that care about such things
will get the information they need from the label.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




