Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Reply-To: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



In article <ubpvxyp253.fsf@kmb07.ing.tu-bs.de>, Stefan Hasselbach <stefan@kmb07.ing.tu-bs.de> writes:

>is there a way to repair BAD BLOCKS detected with fsck on ULTRIX - 32.

Sure, identify what file the bad block belongs to and restore the
file from a backup.  In all likelyhood, the block is bad and the
data in it CAN NOT be recovered or repaired.  If was, some of the
I/O subsystem that ULTRIX supports would have done so and replaced
the block with a good one.

So, taking the above step by step.

1.  Get the block number and normalize for the partition offset.
    If the partition happens to start at the beginning of the
    you can skip this step.  Look at the partition table using
    chpt and note the start of the partition where the block
    was.  Add the offset to the block number given by fsck and
    this is the LBN of the disk.

2.  On an MSCP disk (rd or ra device name), the block has probably
    already been replaced, but MSCP has a feature that allows the
    block to remember that it has corrupt data in it.  The feature
    is called a forced error.  You can clear the FE flag using the
    radisk command.  Check the manual page for the options.

    On a SCSI disk, you can use rzdisk to force a replacement of
    the block (assuming the disk supports that; most should).  On
    V4.4 you can use scu(8) and the "reassign lba" command.

3.  Now, you should have a good block, but since the original data
    couldn't be read, corrupt data.  You need to track down where
    the block was.  Use icheck:

	# icheck -b BN /dev/rr{whatever}

    This will scan the file system to see where that block is
    used.  Use the original block number given by fsck, since
    icheck expects partition relative block numbers.  If the
    block is in a file, the file's inode number will be given
    as well as the position in the file.  If the file is in a
    file system data structure that will also be given.

    For an inode, you can backtrack to the name(s) using ncheck:

	# ncheck -i inode /dev/rr{whatever}

    Once you know the file you can restore it from backup or
    the system distribution.

    The file happens to be a directory, the directory is
    probably corrupt.  Run fsck to relink any unattached files
    to lost+found.  All the files in the directory may be
    unattached, so you may have to create the directory and
    go through lost+found and rename all the files.

    The block was in metadata, it probably left the file system
    corrupt in other ways, but fsck can make the repairs that
    are possible.  If a range of inodes was corrupted, you'll
    want to back-track all those to names, so you can restore
    them if necessary.  

>  Stefan Hasselbach   eMail: stefan@kmbserv1.ing.tu-bs.de
--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




