In article <5e2p03$o0u1@worf.qntm.com>, mdodge@qntm.com (Mike ) writes:

:Can anyone tell me how to change the SCSI ID # for the built-in NCR narrow SE 
:host on an DEC 3000/600 ?    I'd like to change it from 7 to 6.    I imagine 
:this is done by modifying a driver parameter file.   Is this correct?...

   Most Alpha and most DEC 3000-class systems have one or more
   environment variables accessable at console level that are
   used to contain the host ID for each available SCSI bus...

:Also, is there any way to change which host is assigned which bus number?
:Is it possible to switch busses 0 and 1?

   Sure.  Use logical names -- having software that directly
   `cares' about the particular SCSI controller ordering is
   probably the wrong approach...  (It's probably tied into
   the specific hardware configuration a little too tightly.)

   (If you are looking to configure a VMScluster using a SCSI
   interconnect, seriously consider upgrading to OpenVMS V7.1,
   which adds port-level allocation class support -- this greatly
   simplifies configuring the SCSI interconnect in a VMScluster.)

