In article <5eca5b$fmc$1@sys10.cambridge.uk.psi.net>, ihardcas@azlan.co.uk (Iain Hardcastle) writes:
> Hi All,
> I have a collection of DEC hosts and other in the garage. I have,
> however, only one VT. Having been given a decserver 200, is it
> possible to connect all the console's to the DS200 and do a reverse
> LAT or reverse TELNET to each port over a network. I understand that
> the DS200 may not support IP but LAT is quite acceptable at home.
> all help appreciated
> iain
> ihardcas@azlan.co.uk
> 
Hello,
	I did this with a decserver300 when I was on the bench when I had 
only 3 terminals to control the console ports of four translan bridges,
pdp11/24, Vax750,vax8200,microvax2000 and the RA81 maintanence port.
(how do you HANG an RA81 = send XOFF to maintenance port. Does it also kill 
RA82s?)

The choice of DS300 was because of the high number of devices to control in 
the work station, however, if you have seven(or less)  devices to control
then the DS200 should fit the bill; PROVIDED there is one machine on the
Ethernet which can download the DS200 software after a powerfail.
If you do proceed it may be advised to connect the DS200 to a UPS .
(Blackouts are not unknown in south-east Queensland)
A side benefit from this configuration was that of automatic baud-rate 
conversion.(ra81=300,vaxes&pdp=9600,translans=19k2?)

Just setup a Reverse-LAT service on each device port and configure the port
as a "printer" port. 
From the terminal port's Local> prompt SHOW SERVICES and CONNECT as you 
would any other LAT service.

Regards,
	Peter



