Subject: Re: Lockdirwt???
Date: 1997/02/19
Message-ID: <1997Feb19.195449.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 220029585
References: <01bf411d$256200e0$bb4be0c7@dschultz>
Organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <01bf411d$256200e0$bb4be0c7@dschultz>, 
    			"David L. Schultz" <dschultz@epix.net> writes:
> We have an Alpha 7720 clustered with 3 smaller VAX's.  I
>  noticed Lockdirwt set at 1 for one VAX, the other 2 VAX's set at 7 and the
>  Alpha 7720 set at 3... Autogen recommends setting of 1 for the Alpha 7720.
>  I do not have the documentation for Open VMS... Can anyone tell me what
> the
>  Lockdirwt parm means and how this setting can be affecting performance...
>  Also, do you have any tips on what to look for for performance in a
>  clustered environment between Alpha and VAX hosts.

        The LOCKDIRWT sysgen parameter affects  which nodes in a cluster
    will  "master"  lock  trees.  I don't have all the details  at  hand
    (heard  it  explained  a  couple  of  times,  can't  reproduce   the
    discussion),  but  the parameter itself is used as a "weight" in the
    mathematical sense.  The higher the value of the LOCKDIRWT, the more
    lock trees, relative to other  nodes  in the cluster, that the given
    node will, or is likely to, master.

        Two rules of thumb are (1) satellite nodes should have LOCKDIRWT
    set to 0  (zero),  and  (2)  _usually_,  all  boot nodes should have
    LOCKDIRWT  set to 1 (one).  There are cases in which your boot nodes
    have very different performance,  or  asymmetric  access  to  disks,
    etc.,  for  which  you'd  want  to "unbalance" this parameter on the
    affected nodes to force the more powerful node to handle most of the
    lock trees.  Without knowing  which  models of VAX you're clustering
    the  Alpha with, nor how the storage is accessed (CI, DSSI,  SCSI?),
    it's very hard to make a recommendation.

        FWIW, we have a very asymmetric cluster, a VAX 6420, a VAX 6610,
    a VAX  6620,  and  an  Alpha  4100,  all  on  the  CI  (plus  30-odd
    VAXstations  on the NI).  We have LOCKDIRWT=1 on all four  CI-nodes,
    and =0 on all NI nodes.  This allows even the "weak" 6420 to  master
    locks for the disks its MSCP serving to the satellites.

        I'd suggest that  you  need  a  convincing  analysis  to say you
    really _need_ to set the "other 2 VAX's" LOCKDIRWT to 7.  Otherwise,
    set'em all back to 1.  YMMV.

        -Ken
