Uri Raz wrote:
>   It seems to me like DEC has decided OpenVMS is dead, and Windows NT is the
>   future, and migrates the needed functionality from VMS to WNT in accordance
>   with that attitude.

This decision was made very early during Mr Palmer's reign. Dump VMS and
go for the trendy stuff. Mr Palmer saw VMS as a liability because it was
labeled "proprietary".

Once Digital's marketing efforts to move folks away from VMS started to
materialise, and DEC financials took a serious beating BECAUSE OF THAT,
Mr Palmer awoke and realised that he couldn't cut off his legs until he
grew new limbs.

So, if NT's clustering is 10 years behind that of VMS, and lets assume
that it will take at least 5 years for it to catch up, IF IT CAN, then
there is no reason to stop pushing VMS until NT truly replaces it.

Digital will only hurt itself by pissing off VMS customers and hoping
they go NT even though NT still doesn't replace VMS.



