Subject: Re: 33MHz bus?
Date: 1997/02/20
Message-ID: <5eh48a$k7j@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 220098014
References: <01bc1d28$b7a56b20$d89fc9c7@aengus.jlc.net> <5easpj$bo2$1@news.destek.net> <01bc1d37$06526080$8f9fc9c7@aengus.jlc.net>
Organization: CEC Karlsruhe
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <01bc1d37$06526080$8f9fc9c7@aengus.jlc.net> "Daniel Mannarino" <aengus@jlc.net> writes:
>> <remarks about 66 Mhz PCI adapters deleted>
>No, I mean, if the cards are *really* PCI 2.1 compliant (which most are
>advertised as), then they should run fine at 66 (or whatever) MHz.

You don't understand the SPEC. If a card is PCI 2.1 compliant it means
that if you plug it into a (hypothetical) 66 Mhz PCI bus, it will correctly
signal whether it is a 66 Mhz or 33 Mhz device. If a *single* card
on the PCI is 33 Mhz, the whole bus goes down to 33 Mhz. 

No vendor I know of (including those that lobbied for the 66 Mhz extension
in the first place...) ship systems with 66 Mhz capable PCI bus controllers.
About each and every card that's out there today is 33 Mhz. So there would
be no point in a 66 Mhz bus. Heck, there is even a dearth of cards for
33 Mhz/64 bit PCI slots, though now finally there are a few I/O devices
that need that kind of bandwidth (such as FibreChannel controllers, OC-12
ATM adapters and the like).

>  So why does DEC not have 66MHz PCI bus boards?

Because 

	a) there would be nothing to plug into	
	b) we have plenty of bandwidth with 64bit/33 Mhz slots in
	   our workstation and server products
	c) 33 Mhz PCI is electrically challenging enough as it stands,
	   66 Mhz is *very* hard, which is why no card vendor offers
	   products in that space.

> It would allow you to make better use of those DIMM boards too.

In what way ? PCI is I/O bus, the DIMMs form the main memory and have
nothing to do with the I/O busses.

> BTW, I might be missing YOUR point, because
>I do not understand your reference to parity.  What do you mean?

One of the hard things of the timing of PCI devices is the generation 
of the PCI bus parity signals. It is nearly impossible to be fast
enough for 33Mhz PCI with normal FPGA technology, so interface designers
have to rely on custom silicon for those parts.

