 <fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu> wrote:
>        I'd suggest that  you  need  a  convincing  analysis  to say you
>    really _need_ to set the "other 2 VAX's" LOCKDIRWT to 7.  Otherwise,
>    set'em all back to 1.  YMMV.

I dunno -- back in a former life, we had a cluster consisting of an 8550,
8530 and an 8200.  The 8550 ran the accounting package, which absolutely
pounded the lock manager.  When it crashed, the locks got re-mastered on
the other systems, and with the slow 8200 in the loop, we noticed.  We
mostly fixed this by setting LOCKDIRWT roughly according to relative
power, with a slight weight toward the 8550, ie 8550:8, 8530:4, 8200:1.

Even set this way, if the 8530 crashed, followed by the 8550, performance
would suck until we found a quiet time to reboot the 8200 which would
have collected more than its fair share.  Eventually we gave up on the
stupid little thing and set LOCKDIRWT to 0 on the 8200, which fixed the
sucky performance problem.  But we left the large values in place on the
big machines.

