In article <3309C45C.1FEE@concentric.net>, Stuart Brook <sbrook@concentric.net> writes:
> Well, hello q!  Long time!
> 
> The passage of VMS is mirroring the PDP-11 ... and that hasn't gone away
> in any kind of hurry!
> 
> Stuart
-- 
	Agreed - mostly.  Let me mention my experience in Australia.

DEC Oz started winding down on PDP-11 sales and support in the early 80s,
long before USA and UK (or so it seemed from here). 

The reason - they needed the smart techo staff to support VMS, hence
update the good support people. And now I guess the VMS support staff 
will be required to support NT in its development stages. Never enough
experts around when you need them - ie when a product is young and the
customers are new to it.

Now - why did the PDP-11s survive so long ?   Because they were so good.!

They were essentially engineering/scientific boxes used/run by techos.
By the time the VAXs were taking over there was plenty of PDP-11 expertise 
out in user land. The proof is in the DECUS Library.

There was also plenty of technical information about the hardware and the
software - indeed DEC encouraged third party products for PDP-11s. But
they discouraged them for the VAXs. And of course the PDP-11 hardware was
designed to last for 10 years or so. Ken Olsen promised to support them 
forever! (no wonder he had to go!). By comparison, VAX owners have been
encouraged/forced to upgrade both hardware and software to stay supported
and DEC has adopted shorter and shorter product lifecycles.

With VMS running on ALPHA boxes there is no real reason to keep VAX boxes
beyond their commercial lifetime, whereas with many PDP-11 applications
there was no simple way to port the users to alternate hardware.

AS for the AFFINITY program - it sounds good, but if it is going to take
over 10 years to get it all together then there will be a lot of falling
by the wayside in that timeframe. Users need real support all the time
to give them the confidence to stay with DEC, not just promises of a happy 
ending in 2010.



