c'est moi (this@is.me) wrote:
: I heard about some place that has managed to make
: a 8088 compatible chip running at > 2000MHz. 

I think you mean 200 Mhz.  A Pentium Pro is 8088 compatible.  All this 
means is that it can run 8088 software.  So, it can run DOS.

: Considering
: that most of the world's software is still 16 bit, my
: question is would this be faster running legacy code 
: than the new 21264 based workstations we have heard 
: about?

Yes, a Pentium is faster at running DOS 16 bit apps than an Alpha.  Or, 
at least, my Pentium 120 is faster at running Scorched Earth than my 
Alpha 21064.  That's because the Alphas run the 16 bit apps under 
emulation.  But, I don't believe that there are more DOS apps than 
Windows 32 bit apps on the market.  

:  Does linux run on a 8088? 

no.  It switches into protected mode, which is no longer 8088 compatible.

: Is there native DOS for the alpha, and does it have all features such as
: xcopy, etc?

Not really.  Windows NT can run 16 bit DOS and Windows apps.  It has all 
(*cough*) features.  Linux on x86 can run DOSEmu.  I'm not sure if it 
works under the Alpha.

