          [Editor note:  I am *NOT* making this up.    ]

A few months ago, I got this nice little glass candy jar in the mail
from Digital, stuffed with hard candy.   The jar sports the standard
DIGITAL logo on the front.

The enclosed card read:

---------------------------------------------
"    Digital is currently implementing
     a new strategy to deliver improved
        services to our customers.

          Look for updates
    ...and candy refills each month
      ...to keep your jar full.

(signed)  Digital Multivendor Customer Service
        Western States Territory
--------------------------------------------

(Did anyone else get this?)

I find the whole thing amusing, given the following:

* The jar came with standard mints (so far, so good).  But then,....
* In late November, candy corn showed up 
  [keep in mind Halloween was two weeks past, by this point]
* In Mid-January, the christmas-colored mints showed up 
  [again, AFTER the holidays were all over]
* And now, February 18th, cinnamon red-hots showed up
  [4 days PAST valentines day, and the bag was ripped open besides]


Although the concept was cute, I have found it hilarious that their
little "candy upgrades" have ALWAYS been late (as far as the holidays
are concerned).  

I guess, from a pure customer point of view, Digital hasn't done much
with this little gig to impress me that they can deliver their products 
on TIME.

On the assumption that this continues, I honestly expect something like
the following in the mail next month:


========================================================================
"Dear valued Customer,

As an owner of the Digital Multivendor Services Candy Dispenser System
[DEC part number DECCDS-AA], you are being sent a patch to repair
a known flaw.     The attached DSNlink article describes the issue:

                     Signed, 
                          Shards O'Glass
                          Digital Multivendor Customer Services Representative
-----------------------------------------------------------------

[DEC-CANDY] Candy jar falls to floor causing really loud CRASH
Last Technical Review: 18-FEB-1997              Size: 42 lines


     Any party granted access to the following copyrighted information
     (protected under Federal Copyright Laws), pursuant to a duly executed
     Digital Service Agreement may, under the terms of such agreement copy
     all or selected portions of this information for internal use and
     distribution only. No other copying or distribution for any other
     purpose is authorized.

Copyright (c) Digital Equipment Corporation 1997. All rights reserved.

PRODUCT:  Digital Multivendor Customer Services Candy Dispenser System V1.0

OP/SYS:   DECCDS-desktop

SOURCE:   Digital Equipment Corporation

SYMPTOM:

Under certain circumstances, the desktop base unit of the DECCDS-AA has been 
known to easily slip off the desktop and drop to the floor, causing an 
immediate CRASH.       


DIGITAL RESPONSE:

Digital Multivendor Customer Services Engineering has acknowledged this 
problem and plans to address it in a future release.


WORKAROUND:

Apply the enclosed tempory patch (Dec part number DECCDS-RBR-DSK)
To apply the patch, shutdown the system (empty the contents into a backup
container), remove the adhesive, and apply to the middle of the underside of
