From: Boris Gjenero <bgjenero@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca>
Subject: Twisted solution to my RD53 problem
Date: 1997/02/19
Message-ID: <330A731A.40076198@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 219782737
Sender: news@watserv3.uwaterloo.ca
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: The First Frontier
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
X-Mailer: Mozilla 3.01Gold (X11; I; Linux 2.0.27 i586)


I've finally managed to get one of my RD53 drives to work.  I can't say
I repaired it, but I can definitely say that I persuaded it to work.  I
did open the drive and put in the piece of paper over the rubber bumper.
The heads wre sticking mildly, and it probably wasn't a problem.  That
is because the drive behaved in *exactly* the same way when the "fix"
was done.  I even opened up the drive again and verified the fix.

I did see that if the head movement actuator was disconnected when the
drive reached operating RPM and for a couple of seconds afterwards and
then connected, then the drive would work.  So, I built a small
electronic circuit to connect the head actuator with a relay after the
drive was at operating RPM for a few seconds.  This works very reliably,
and the drive is perfectly healthy (except for this hack).
I'm concerned that by using normally open relay contacts I'm defeating
the head parking, but I can modify the circuit to fix that.

I wasn't as lucky with the other drive.  I managed to persuade it to
spin, but it seems to be one big bunch of bad sectors.  I get repeated
seek sounds and then humming.  I might try a low level format
eventually, but I doubt that will work.
BTW.  This drive also had the heads totally glued to the bumper.  I had
to apply a lot of force and watch the bumper stretch.

I've found that the RD53 doesn't spin down at once if the heads are
stuck. You'll hear some humming as it tries to move the heads.  It'll
stay at operating RPM for some seconds before giving up.  This wasn't
what was happening to me.

Also, when the motor pulses at operating RPM, that means that the drive
is unable to lock onto the servo information which should be coming from
a disk surface dedicated to servo information.  Curiously, the second
(non-working) drive does this if the head actuator is connected at spin
up.  

WARNING:  Do not do any of these things to a drive if you actually want
to trust it.  It works for me (so far), but I don't know how much longer
it will.

