In article <3304FFB3.76A63FF2@esslink.com> supernaut <suprnaut@esslink.com> writes:
>Well, the official minimum cache size spec is 2mb.  
> The DEC rcomended cache sizes are in the range of 2-8mb.

There is no such thing as "official minimum cache size" for Alphas.
Machines like the Personal Workstation 433a and 500a come with no
board level caches at all and they perform just fine. Whether any
particular floating point application benefits from 2Mbyte of cache
depends on the memory footprint of the application. If it fits
in 1 Mbyte fine (all of SPECint95 is happy with 1 Mbyte). If
it doesn't fit in 1 Mbyte but fits in 2, you win. If it doesn't
fit in either 1 or 2, you waste money. SPECfp95 has some candidates
that benefit from caches in the range of 1 Mbyte to 16 Mbyte. Whether
any of *your* codes does, needs to be checked. For things like
Softimage and the like, the cache sizes don't seem to matter much.

> Cache is much more important in
>an Alpha machine than an intel one.  That doesn't mean you need to buy a
>machine with 2mb, but make sure it's upgradable to 2mb or more.

We may need a little more cache than Intel, but generally I would just
say that a good overall memory hierarchy obviously is more important
in faster machines than slower machines (regardless of Intel/Alpha or
whatever). Anyhow, I don't think 2 Mbyte is anything magic (unless your
FP codes match SPECfp95 very close).

