shannon@world.std.com wrote:
>DEC didn't have to sell OVMS to u$oft. u$oft managed to obtain, through
>Dave Cutler, a new and improved OVMS which is called Windows NT.

This seems overstated at best.  What are the distinguishing features of
VMS?  Its process model, the variety and quantity of system services
(provided in several different manners), DCL, and the way everything has
a design model (Bell/LaPadula security, 7-layer OSI networking, etc).

NT has none of this that is detectable, except maybe the quantity of
Win32 function calls.  Nobody knows (except Microsoft and the privileged
few who've read NT source) what NT's process lifecycle is, NT's command
line is inherited more-or-less unchanged from MS-DOS, security is on a
"trust us, We're Microsoft!" basis, and the preferred mode of doing
network stuff is SMB.  The Win32 functions owe more to Unix than anything
else, and the only "internals" docs ("Inside Windows NT") seems to have
NT being a clone of Mach 2.0 (no arbitrary pager support).



