Thanks for looking:

I have a DEC AlphaStation 200 4/100 that I was using for a server for a
small ISP I once had.  I've been unable to sell the system at a price that
I think is deserving so I decided to check on the possiblity of converting
it into a workstation.

I was thrilled to learn of FX|32.  I took a look and went out and bought a
copy of WinNT4.0.

Everything was great until......

1.  I was never issued a copy of the program CD that allows a user to
upgrade his/her firmware.  The system came pre-loaded with Digital UNIX 3.2
which I love; but, it is very impractical for workstation software.  My
reseller told me that I would have to buy a copy of Unix for the system. 
That was the first time in my life as a network administrator that I wasn't
furnished a copy of the operating system my machine was working on.  I
thought it strange at the time. Now I think that it is down right
criminal!!!  But that's another story.

2.  This upgrade allows me to convert from the SRM console to the ARC
console which is needed for NT.

--------------------------------------------
3.  Can someone tell me how to get this???
--------------------------------------------

4.  Second,  the floppy drive failed in the machine.  When I finally
restarted the system and got an error message about a device, I realized
that the case is locked and I don't have a key to open the back.  Has this
ever happened to anyone.  What steps did you take to correct this short of
a cutting torch?


It is really a shame that I don't have any more faith in Digital than I do.
 Their support people are very helpful; but when you really need them they
aren't there for you.  I installed 4 big servers in my school district and
decided against Digital because of problems like I experienced.  I'm
determined that I am not going to scrap this AlphaStation.  Digital has
been advertizing the interoperability of NT with UNIX and I want to test
their marketing.
