Bruce Ediger wrote:
> 
> shannon@world.std.com wrote:
> >DEC didn't have to sell OVMS to u$oft. u$oft managed to obtain, through
> >Dave Cutler, a new and improved OVMS which is called Windows NT.
> 
> This seems overstated at best.  

<Snip>

Or oversimplified. Nevertheless, before leaving Digital, Cutler was
working on a VMS-like OS for the PRISM RISC architecture. Said OS was
called MICA. MICA was to have been a platform-independent open system
that provided VMS run-time libraries, user environment, management
interface, et al.

A memo on MICA obtained by my dear friend and colleague Charlie Matco
revealed that Cutler "was reimplementing VMS, fixing known bugs, and
incorporating features from Unix..."

Now, I've never seen MICA source code, but there are people in Digital
who have told me that certain sections of MICA and WNT are identical,
right down to the comments.

Whether or not this is true, Cutler obviously was able to draw on a
large VMS- (and VAXeln)-specific knowledge base when implementing WNT.



