References: <854751032.25487@dejanews.com> <32F351B0.29D7@ACM.ORG> <32F3E740.267F@videotron.ca> <32F4B658.5FBB@world.std.com> <5d2h7o$nd6@rzsun02.rrz.uni-hamburg.de> <32F4D57F.B20@world.std.com>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: SPC
Mime-Version: 1.0
Reply-To: "[nospam]jfmezei"@videotron.ca
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.alpha,comp.org.decus
X-Mailer: Mozilla 3.0 (Macintosh; I; PPC)


One aspect that has changed with regards to VMS is Digital's babysitting
of VMS.

In the days of Digital recognizing the value of VMS, it made serious
attempts to bring software to VMS, and if nobody did, Digital would do
it itself. As a result, VMS was an application rich environment. It had
connectivity with IBM systems, job scheduling with pager alerts if
something went wrong, rock solid messaging systems and the list goes on.

Digital was able to fill the gaps that third parties didn't fill and
hence keep a critical mass of software applications on VMS that made VMS
an attractive system.

With Digital's pullout of application software (or it silencing of
marketing for the later), VMS is left to fend for itself.

Meanwhile, one the the paradigm shifts (I hate that expression but heck,
it applies here :-) was the shift away from homegrown software to shrink
wrapped software. Another one was for software developpers to shift from
developping on systems with a serious installed base to developping
systems that showed the most growth potential.

As a result of all of this, Digital has pulled the rug out of its own
VMS applications, and the hints that VMS was dying made software
developpers shift from a future-less VMS towards UNIX and now NT.



