Thanks to those who responded to my inquiry on Alpha clones.
(In reference to the word, it is not *totally* clear to me that
the motherboard is always from Digital -- some vendors appear
to have *implied* to me that they make the board themselves).  

I haven't heard any bad stories about the mail-order vendors yet.
I have heard speculation that the Digital compiler working with
DU will likely be faster than Linux and some other compiler
(Microway, NAG, g77, etc.), which I had actually taken as a given, 
but no one has come forward with actual numbers.  If I go through 
with a Linux system, I'll have some numbers myself (I already have
a pure Digital machine) and I'll post 'em here.

The cache is a puzzle -- most vendors tell me the 1 Mb upper
limit is built into the board, but Microway has a 2 Mb board they
sell.  Obviously if I have a program that fits into 1 Mb I win,
but I'm guessing a nontrivial number of mine won't.  I don't 
understand the few comments I've gotten to the effect that for 
programs that don't fit entirely into cache the size of the cache
is irrelevant.  I would have guessed quite the opposite, that a 
larger cache results in fewer cache misses a priori, and so will 
benefit any program, except for some ridiculous monster which 
does vector operations with a stride of 10 000. Any hardware 
geeks care to come to my rescue here and enlighten me?

I'm still interested in anyone's experience out there with the
Alpha "clones" from Enorex, ASL, etc., especially with scientific
or engineering computation (i.e. I don't care how fast or even if
Corel Draw or a commercial database engine works).  If you want 
to e-mail me your comments directly remove the _ in the adddress 
above. I'll summarize any info I get to the NG. Thanks.

Christopher J. Grayce
UCI Department of Chemistry



