> > Lord knows the commodity market seems to have a hard time generating
> > PCI parity at 33 MHz, never mind 66 MHz....
> >
> > --
> > Dave Cherkus ------- UniMaster, Inc. ------ Contract Software Development
> > Specialties: UNIX Internals/Kernel TCP/IP Alpha Clusters Performance ISDN
> > Email: cherkus@UniMaster.COM  When the music's over, turn out the lights!
> No, I mean, if the cards are *really* PCI 2.1 compliant (which most are
> advertised as), then they should run fine at 66 (or whatever) MHz.  So why

  The 64 bit and 66 MHz configurations are *options* in PCI 2.1;  there is no
  requirement that these have to be implemented (good thing too!).  The 64 bit
  and 66 MHz options are alternative approaches to doubling peak bandwidth to
  266 MB/s (or if used together, obtain 533 MB/s peak BW).  Of the two, going
  to 64 bits is far easier in my experience than shooting for 66 MHz.  This is
  part of the reason that Intel defined the AGP (point to point instead of
  bussed, and Tval(max) relaxed from 6 to 7 ns) instead of waiting for 66 MHz
  PCI video adaptors.  64 bit, 33 MHz PCI is better than 32 bit 66 MHz PCI
  because 1) it easier to meet the timing, and 2) better mixing of standard
  32/33 PCI boards with higher bandwidth cards - a 64 bit target and initiator
  can negotiate 64 bit transfers even with the presence of 32 bit cards on
  the bus segment; - in a 66 MHz system a bus segment has to always run at 33
  MHz if even one 33 MHz card is present.

> does DEC not have 66MHz PCI bus boards?  It would allow you to make better
> use of those DIMM boards too.  BTW, I might be missing YOUR point, because

  The CPU doesn't access DRAM over PCI; it is an I/O bus, ok?

  In the high end systems like a DEC 8400 the system bus has much higher
  bandwidth than even 64 bit, 66 MHz PCI.  The solution is to support
  multiple PCI bus segments instead (up to 144 IIRC) so that multiple I/O
  operations can be performed concurrently.

> I do not understand your reference to parity.  What do you mean?

  The timing spec to XOR together the 32 data bits transmitted in cycle N and
  output as parity in cycle N+1 can be hard to meet.

> 
>         Daniel

  Paul

