In article <slrn5gf1jc.rhj.adisaacs@pepperoni.csl.mtu.edu>, adisaacs@mtu.edu (Andy Isaacson) writes:

> RedHat (http://www.redhat.com/) makes a Linux distribution for Alpha-based
> systems.  They do not have a product for MIPS-based systems (at least, not
> yet.)  There is a project to port Linux to the MIPS processor; you can read
> about it at http://www.fnet.fr/linux-mips/.  Looks like it's still pretty
> rudimentary, so be prepared to hack.
 
> I believe that NetBSD has been ported to the MIPS.  I haven't used NetBSD
> at all, so I can't tell you anything about it.  You can find more info at
> http://www.netbsd.org/.

I'm still officially in charge of NetBSD/pmax, so I'm clearly biased, but:

One very good reason to use Linux is the very large range of supported
hardware, and the large body of users and expertise with Linux -- on
x86 hardware, at least.  But for DECstation hardware, the free Unix OS
with the largest such body of users and expertise *is* (IMHO) NetBSD,
(and/or OpenBSD),  not Linux.

It's not clear how much of the expertise (and code base) in the Linux
community is really useful for DECstations or DECstation users.
DECstations don't really have any I/O devices in common with the x86,
or even with the (non-Turbochannel) Alpha systems that Linux supports.

DECstation users are also likely to be more familiar with a
BSD-derived and sysadmin environment, like NetBSD, than with
System-V-like Linux distributions like Red Hat.



