My experience with DEC is similar.

We wanted a pair of Alphastations, which at the time
were the best buy, bang/buck.

Simply getting a quote for two Alphastations was "like pulling
teeth".

I was representing a UC Berkeley research group, we've bought
lots of stuff from DEC over the years.

Suddenly, with a change in sales rep, we're PERSONA NON GRATA.
We don't exist, we're not worth generating quotes for,
we're ignored months at a time, and when I do finally reach
the guy and pin him down, it's lame excuses and insincere 
apologies (a sincere apology would have come with a change in
treatment.)

I thought it was just the idiot I was dealing with, but
when I went around him, there were just more idiots at DEC
sales.  Nobody could generate the quotes (with the appropriate
UCB discount) for me!  Not for a few months anyway.

Finally I got the quote I wanted.  

Maybe it isn't worth DEC's time to deal with little sales
like me, but when I DO have $100k to $1M to throw around, am
I going to deal with DEC?  After being treated as I have been?

Also look at the wonderful reputation DEC sales has made for
itself.  All because of little, simple things like NOT MAKING
QUOTES promptly.

Get a clue, DEC:

1)  The "little people" who you screw over screw you back by
telling the world how you treated them, and the "big people"
are VERY sensitive to reputation!

2)  Someone buying small today may be able to buy BIG tomorrow.
Don't poison people against you.

3)  Customers get unhappy when they're kept waiting for quotes.
We CAN and WILL buy elsewhere.

4)  Hiding under a stone when a customer is looking for a DEC sales rep
to talk to WON'T ENHANCE YOUR SALES.

5)  Customers are NOT IMPRESSED when you get the quote they asked
for wrong multiple times.

6)  We're even LESS IMPRESSED when you take several weeks to fix 
a simple mistake in a quote.


If DEC can't even do the simple things, no one will trust them
with the hard things.


Peter Mardahl
Just a "graduate student" but my prof. buys what I recommend!


(P.S., I don't want to hear any apologies from anyone at DEC.
It's a case of too little, too late.  I want DEC to be there
next time I need them.)



