> was looking to spend several hundred thousand $ on a new Alpha cluster.
> DEC's Benchmarking Center in Anaheim was extremely helpful in
> demonstrating that yes, the Alpha was the most cost-effective
> solution for our application.  We *wanted* a VMScluster.  Nevertheless,
> we had to send out competetive bids.  Do you know how hard it was
> to drag a bid out of a Digital salesperson?  It was like pulling
> teeth.  All they had to do was submit a bid on the configuration
> we wanted.  We didn't care what the discount was - DEC was going to
> win in cost effectiveness no matter what, and we didn't want to get
> stuck with another HP/UX or RS6000 non-cluster that was going to be
> a pain in the ass to manage.  Finally - after months of phone calls
> to Digital and Wyle - we got them to submit a bid.  But it turned
> out to be too late for the bidding process.
> 
> Technically, Digital had it then - they had the CPU, they had the
> boxes, they had the OS.  The technical folk at the benchmarking
> center were more than happy to convince us that they had the box
> we wanted.  But were the salespeople willing to sell it?  No.  We
> would've gladly bought at list or our standard discount - all they
> had to do was submit the bid.  But evidently a most-of-a-million-dollars
> sale wasn't a priority for Digital's or its affiliate's salespeople.
> It was damn frustrating.  Especially in light of the way the HP
> and IBM salespeople were bending over backwards to sell us a system -
> giving us discounts we'd never even imagined were possible on leading
> edge technology.  Of course, they had to offer such discounts to be
> compatible with DEC's list price at the time.  But we couldn't even
> get Digital to submit a bid at *list price*!!!
> 
