In article <slrn5g40c4.3pt.viper@nova.campus.luth.se> viper@nova.campus.luth.se.remove.this.extension.to.reply (Mikael Nykvist) writes:

>The one that matters most to me is the memorybandwidth. From what I've
>understood, the 21164 cpu's using DRAM scores about 160M/s in benchmarks
>like stream (is this result with 128bit or 256bit mempath?!?), while
>the new SDRAM alphas will score +300M/s from what I've understood. 

The 166 Mbyte/sec. result for the 21171 chip set with DRAM is with a
256 bit memory bus. The "new SDRAM Alphas" are with the 21174 chip
set and at the moment use the 21164a as well, so the CPU doesn't make
the difference. The SDRAM memory data path in these boxes (the Personal
Workstation 433a and 500a) is 128 bit wide.

>Though, from what I've understood the SDRAM
>alphas will _not_ have 2nd or 3rd level cache, which seems like a bad
>move.

Every 21164a has 2 levels of cache on chip. The 21164PC has one level.
You can get the SDRAM based Alpha with or without a 3rd level cache.
The order number for a 2 Mbyte board level cache is SN-MSC01-AA.

See

	http://www.digital.com/info/SOHOME/SOHOMTPF.PDF

for the complete description of these machines.

>* How much speed diff between 1 and 2M cache for general usage? (like
>  gcc compiling)

There is virtually no difference for GCC compiling. The SPECint95
suite contains GCC as one of the benchmarks and the difference between
1 and 2 Mbyte cache is less than 3%.

