In comp.os.vms Linwood Ferguson <Ferguson@mag.aramark.com> wrote:
> I have a similar but different problem.  We are now using a DECserver
> 250 and want to switch to an IP capable device.

  Didn't we have this discussion elsewhere, or am I in a time vortex again?

  Have you considered protocol translation? (Can a Cisco running Enterprise
do this for you?)

> The DECserver 250 did not have significant buffers, it was more or less
> just like being driven from an LP11 controller.  We tried a Lantronix
> EPS2, also a network device, which supports raw stream printing.  It
> didn't work; turns out you needed an "active" cable (really a separate
> centronix -> dataproducts converter from a company called Photologic,
> about $320, plus the $450 or so for the EPS2). We decided not to use it,
> but that is an option. Just seemed a little too rube-goldberg like.  I
> *think* done that way there is no significant buffering.  Of course you
> need something to write stream IP to it, like Multinet.

  Is that LANtronix's story these days? They used to say that you needed to
open the LPxx and cut out all the pullup/pulldown resistors, which was even
worse. I just added some 7407's to the EPS2 (inside the case) to add enough
drive - the default EPS just connects the outputs of their semi-custom IC to
the DB-25 connector (blech!).

> We are going to try next a serial -> parallel converter from Black Box.
> You can get small and big buffer versions (I think the small is 4k). 
> With the small it is little enough buffered to be usable, if still
> annoying (we use quite a few of these for some old LP26's).  My concern
> with the LP29 is throughput - it only takes 38,400 baud, and even if it
> keeps up with 38.4k that's marginal for 2000lpm (which it may or may not
> - lots of things go slower than their baud rate, like VT terminals).

  Remember that the rated speeds of Dataproducts printers depend on the
character set in use and the band pattern, and that the band speed actually
*changed* in a later firmware release on the LP25/26! (yes, it's under soft-
ware control). The true speed is often less than the advertised speed (which
is the maximum). Use the self-test to do the repeating barberpole and con-
tinuous character tests and time either 30 seconds and cound pages or time
10 pages and count seconds to compute the actual speed. Take the slower of
the 2 values.

> The LP26 printers had a dataproducts-supplied serial card for them, that
> DEC would install and support.  I haven't checked for the LP29; it was
> awfully expensive on the LP26; we ended up buying the Black Box
> converters.  You could buy one and a spare for what Dataproducts wanted.

  You could also get a Centronics interface for the printer, which gives
you more options.

  I've also done things with 7406's on the Dataproducts printers to make
them speak Centronics (I thought the card was overpriced too). As I recall,
you need to invert the strobe line, data 1-8 go to data 0-7, make sure the
high bit is zero unless your printer supports "paper instruction", and
the DP "demand" line goes to the Centronics "ack". You also need to cross-
wire some of the status signals (online, paper out, etc.). The exact details
vary depending on what sort of box is driving the Centronics interface (PC's
are trivial, some older mainframe/minis were pickier).

> If you hear of other alternatives I'd like to know as well.  

  Just consider a 4KB buffer when you get somebody sending a bunch of binary
garbage (including vertical tab/skip to undefined channel/form feeds). 4KB
of formfeeds is 1.3 boxes of lineprinter paper 8-)

