While the SRBs do use a solid fuel (composed of some rather nasty
rubber/Aluminum/perchlorate mixtures), the shuttle main engines use
a mix of liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen.  The main engines are
fed the liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen from the external fuel tank
(That big red thing in the middle of the stack).  Of course, the
external tank is divided into two compartments.

If you'll watch the close-ups of the launch, you'll note that the three
shuttle main engines are started about 7 seconds before the scheduled
liftoff in order to give them time to be ramped up to full power (and to
check for any anomalies).  The flame from these engines is a pale blue
color (The sparks you see during the ignition are from pyrotechnic devices
to prevent any hydrogen buildup during the ignition sequence).  If there's
a problem during the main engine throttle up, the engines can be shut down
and the launch aborted.  At a few tenths of a second before liftoff, the
SRBs are ignited, and there's no turning back (e.g., it's going somewhere
at that point).  The flame from the SRBs is a brilliant reddish-white color
and is partially obscured by the smoke clouds from them.

Dave




