Ketil Z Malde (ketil@imr.no) wrote:
: duraid@fl.net.au (Duraid Madina) writes:

: > Oops! Which reminds me. Suppose I'm dual booting NT/UNIX. Any way I
: > can get them to share a partition? Does Dec UNIX have / would it be
: > possible to write support for FAT or even NTFS? Dammit looks like I'll
: > need a file server to transfer data across :)

: Heh...well, I believe Linux will read FAT/NTFS, but whether it works on
: Alpha, I don't know.

Linux runs on Alpha fairly well.  It _does_ support FAT.  I don't know, 
but I believe you can run Linux on a FAT partition via umsdos, but it's 
not suggested.  The way I've got it set up, I have NT boot from a 100 meg 
FAT partition, but run out of a 1.9 Gig NTFS partition (both on the same 
2 Gig drive).  Linux runs off a ext2 file system, but mounts the 100 Meg 
FAT partition for transferring data between the two.

As for NTFS support, I doubt it.  I have it running on my Intel Linux 
box, so I can grab thongs out of my NT partition.  It's not perfect, and 
doing heavy operations (ie, grep foo `find /nt`) doesn't always work.  I 
wouldn't count on it for anything too important. 

Can DU mount FAT or NTFS partitions?  I think it's the same as Linux.  
FAT is the defacto floppy standard.  It's actually damn fine for 
floppies, where the data is temporary and you don't want such niceties as 
security and caching.  So, DU probably supports the filesystem type, 
since it already needs it for floppies.  And the GNU mtools have been 
ported, which you could probably fake into using your hard drive instead 
of a floppy.  NTFS support I doubt.

