> Sorry, but it ain't just marketing.
> 
> It is sales, support and direction.

People who know what they are doing and acutally care about it will
evaluate a product and the company that is selling it on many criteria
before making an important decision.  Yes, they will want a quality
product that is reliable and backed up by an organization that can keep
it working.   In short, they will want a product that they can trust.  

Unfortunately, it seems that most people aren't like that.  They will
buy the most widely advertised or cheapest thing without thinking about
it.  For example, they'd buy an unstable OS that I wouldn't even trust
as a screen saver, and which lacks even the most basic tools.

If a company wants to make a lot of money, marketing is definitely the
way to go.  Who cares about the product.  They just have to convince
people that they should buy it.  If you doubt this, there are many fine
examples of this, such as Microsoft.

Companies like Digital will be around nevertheless, because there is
always a need for their products.  If you doubt this, replace VMS and
Unix systems with PC clones running Windows 95.  Make sure you replace
any  critical servers that *must* stay up all the time (use NT if you
have a slightly lesser deathwish).  
BTW.  Give away all the old hardware...  I'm, sure many people in a.f.c
will love it.

