> from 20 to about 13. Coupla mail-order folks offer
> these, e.g. Enorex, ASL, SWT, for around $6500 if
> you run Linux.

A guy in our department has a 433MHz Alpha running
Linux, 1mb cache.  For highly FP intensive applications
on our code, written in Fortran, this machine
was 2.5 - 3.5 times faster than a 180MHz PPro.
Similar results were seen when compared to a single
SP2 node (RS/6000 model 590).

> 
> A different deal is offerred by Microway, which
> has a *2* Mb cache version for about $2000 more,
> again with Linux.

Depends on the memory footprint of your code.  If it
fits in 2mb but not 1mb, then yes, it will be faster.
If it still doesn't fit in 2mb, then the speed 
increase won't be a substantial.  From the performance
I've seen from models with only 1mb of cache I really
wouldn't lose a whole lot of sleep over it.

Check out http://www.dcginc.com.  A 2mb cache upgrade
is only an extra $800. 

> So, anybody have bad experiences with mail order
> Alpha clones from these guys?  Anybody find running
> Linux a severe disappointment vs. DU?

The only problem with Linux is that lack of compilers.
Finding a Fortran 90 compiler has been a bit of an adventure.
My advice is to get a clone and run Digital UNIX on it.  
The 500MHz clones are selling for about 1/4 the cost of their
Digital bred counterparts (granted, the Digital machines
are somewhat different from the clones, but still, is that
worth an extra $25,000 ?!).

  Anybody think
> 1 Mb -> 2 Mb cache upgrade will not improve typical
> smallish Fortran/C mathematical (e.g. numerical
> integration) code by 20 - 30% depending on program
> size?

In real life, I don't think you're going to see a 30% 
improvement in speed just by moving to a 2mb cache.

Jeff



