>ext2fs *is* limited but the limit is somewhere around a couple of
>terabytes per file system. That's `a couple of thousand gigabytes' for
>you.

>It'll be a while yet before you can connect a disk *that* big to a PC.

You don't need a big disk.  I assume ext2fs supports sparse files.
On a 1 GB file system I can do:

% bigtest
Exp c              hex              decimal m
  4 A               10                   16  
  5 B               20                   32  
  6 C               40                   64  
  7 D               80                  128  
  8 E              100                  256  
  9 F              200                  512  
 10 G              400                 1024 K
 11 H              800                 2048 K
 12 I             1000                 4096 K
...
 30 [         40000000           1073741824 G
 31 \         80000000           2147483648 G
 32 ]        100000000           4294967296 G
 33 ^        200000000           8589934592 G
 34 _        400000000          17179869184 G
...
 42 g      40000000000        4398046511104 T
 43 h      80000000000        8796093022208 T
 44 i     100000000000       17592186044416 T
write-4: File too large

% ls -ls foo
728 -rw-rw-r--   1 werme    system   8796093022210 Feb 11 08:35 foo

The hex/decimal columns are the offsets the program is writing a few
bytes at.  Our UFS limit is 2^44 - 2^13.  2^13 is the FS block size,
it's no coincidence that the above is (2^31 - 1) * 2^13.
