peter@cyklop.volvo.se (peter hakanson) wrote:

:Why do you call this a " catastrofic situation" ?
:Using the same kernel implies using the same hw (which we 
:shurley don't want - that would be to stop development!)

Most of the kernel is hardware independent.
Obviously it would have to be fitted to the different types of
hardware.

:What we do need (and what un*x has delivered) is a defined
:API that the apps can use. 
:
:Well close too, many vendord does their very best to offer
:incompatible "improvments".
Exactly. That's an important part of the problem. They have to get rid
of this *stupid* attitude. These vendors are realy losing a lot on it.
They still haven't understood the demands of the market, that is of
the users.

: But if you avoid them you
:can be portable.
:This is where the posix/unix95 initiative is very welcome.

Yes, it is. But is it powerfull enough by now?

:Moving "well-written apps" around the common un*x varients 
:is no real difficult thing. Moving badly written vendor-specific
:apps is a real pain!

If you look at all the different things Informix support and do not
support on different hardware and OS's you will see that it still
isn't quite as easy.
The gole of *every* supplier of Unix (and the hardware) must be for
this to *allways* be a simple recompile. That's not only possible, but
easy with the right kind of attitude.
There are however several other issues such as what files are used for
what purposes, their format, naming of devices, kernel tuning
parameters and so on. When these are different Unix becomes that much
harder to use. Several of these does of course not directly depend on
the kernel itself, but it would be much easier to get it right through
a cooperation at the kernel level and necessary for parts of it.

:Using NT might look temtating, but here you are totally in
:the hands of Bill Gates. If Bill drops support for something -
:well you are out! Not even ibm can resist that (without
:paying BIG money in Bill's pocket.
:And NT will not be able to handle real memory (read 64bits).
:
:I wont see NT as a real alternativ to real applications 
:until the day a can byu an "NT-compatible os" from another vendor.
:(I might still buy gates, but at least i have competition).

You mentioned NT, I didn't.
This issue is independent of NT.
NT simply makes it more urgent.
Also that you don't see NT as a real alternative isn't the most
interesting issue. The market don't seem to agree with you, and that
is the key as far as this competiton goes.

The point here is that the industry had a unique chance at getting it
right with the move to 64bit Unix, but they are slipping up again.
HP and SCO are at least doing their part by uniting HP/UX, SCO Open
Enterprise and UnixWare into one Unix. If they had also gotten Sun in
on this, the whole Unix community would have come a very long way to
become a realy dominating force. As it is now they will have an uphill
battle to retain their marketshare on large hardware, and limited
possibilities on smaller servers.

The desktop is of course lost a long time ago, due in a large part to
exactly this incompatibility, but it can possibly be partly regained
in a way by the NC.


