[...]
> The software positioned for clustering in a Digital Unix environment
> is TruCluster


        If you want a _true_ cluster, you do a VMScluster.  VMS has been
    doing clusters for 12 or more  years  now.   If you want DSSI or CI,
    _only_  VMSclusters give you these interconnects.  In addition,  you
    may use any or all of: Memory Channel, SCSI (up to three Alpha nodes
    on a bus), FDDI, Ethernet, and I believe ATM  is  supported  in  VMS
    7.1.

        What you get in a Unix cluster  is little more than disk sharing
    (through  NFS or AFS) and failover in the event of one node crashing
    or otherwise being taken  out  of  service.   VMSclusters  give  you
    _direct_,  shared  access  to  all storage (typically, local storage
    need not be  made  cluster  visible),  cluster-wide file locking and
    record locking, up to 96 nodes in a single cluster (will be expanded
    shortly I expect...see thread on Galaxies), a single security domain
    transparent to the users, etc., etc.

        I'll  agree  with  the   two   Digits  who  posted  pointers  to
    TruClusters if you need to do this under (someone's) unix.  I'm sure
    that if anyone can make unix clusters full-featured and reliable, it
    will  be  Digital,  drawing  upon their  many  years  of  VMScluster
    experience.

        However, I'd argue that if you  want 24x365 sort of reliability,
    you'd  do  well to investigate VMS.  CI-based clusters give you  the
    highest performance (today) with HSJ controllers and CIPCA  adapters
    in   your   Alphas.    (Tomorrow   it  will  be  some  variation  on
    ultra-scsi/turbo-channel...whatever they decide to call it...)

        Sorry to intrude, I just  wanted  to inject a little perspective
    into the current "cluster" discussions from someone who's been using
    clusters since 1984...

        -Ken
