I have two VAXstation 2000.  They both seem to have problems with their
RD53 drives.  One of the drives spins up to what seems like operating
RPM and then immediately shuts dows.  However, it occasionally works
(often after not being used for a long time).  The second one spins up,
and then the motor pulses a few times a second.  This regulates the RPM,
but from the other drive (when it worked), it seems that the standard
RPM operation should toggle motor power much faster.  The second drive
shuts down in 50 seconds.

Anyways, I opened both VAXstations (very well designed for disassembly),
and then I took out the hard drives.  Running them in a PC clone
provided the same results.  Then, I unscrewed the main board from the
drive, and I did extensive testing.  I determined that the signal that
was pulsing one motor and shutting doesn the the other one came from the
main board (not the motor controller board).

After spending hours looking at signals with a scope and tracing out
circuits (I have no schematic), I decided to try swapping main and motor
controller boards.  That had no effect--the symptoms stayed with the
drive. (I should have done this first!)  
Therefore the fault lies elsewhere (mechanical?)  :-(
This of course didn't swap motors, but the motors seemed to be fine. 
The feedback from the hall effect sensors was clean, and the windings
were okay.

I concluded that the microcontroller on the drive was shutting it down
because it found a fault.  Is there any way to get error codes out of or
run detailed diagnostics on RD53 drives (I don't think the diagnostics
on the VAXstation are good enough--I need lower level diagnostics)? 
Does anybody recognize this failure mode?  

If all fails, can I just stick any MFM drive in the VS2000 and should
that work?

