>> looking like an office.  If he clicked on a notepad, he got a text
>> processor that put him through unbelievable contortions just to make a
>> note.  If he clicked on the clock he got the time, which he could have
>> done by looking at his watch.  If he clicked on the outbox he could send
>> e-mail to his daughter, whom he could have called.  At least that was
>> supposed to happen ... he didn't know he had to plug it into a phone
>> jack.  Nothing, except possibly the notepad, was the least bit useful in
>> real-estate.
>> 
>> All this for $3K!!!  I'd like to get my hands on the salesman that
>> shamed him into buying it.  I'm aware that the PC is capable of a lot
>> more, but why tell him ... he couldn't use what he had, and what he had
>> was nothing!
>
> I am in agreement with the above posts.  I know a few people that
> absolutly should not even be around computers.  Two of my buddies work 
> for a customer support organization for Windoze based PC's and the 
> stories that they have been telling me are quite hard to believe.  
>
  I recall a programmer that was called in the middle of the night
  in order to release an application that "froze", just to see that
  the user pressed the hold button on the VT (like "Scroll Lock") 
> 
> From the above post it sounds like the "SUCKER" purchaced a Packard-Bell
> from some retail organization or store.  I've seen the interface
> described above.  A Microsoft hack designed to impress the unknowing 
> lemming into the purchace.  A lot of glitz, very little function.  
>
  I think it's all a result of lack of education - computers and computer
  software are complex things, and people's lack of knowledge, combined
  with the attitude of treating computers to be as simple as TVs, videos,
  and telephones (how many videos blink 12:00 all the time ? how many
  people cant tune their TVs ? how many people dont understand all the
  new services supplied by phone companies), and twice as necessary.

  The same attitude may be found on the internet, where people are
  more knowledgable about technology - how many times did you see
  people asking questions they know should be in the FAQ, but dont
  know where to find it ? people who look for NGs discussing certain
  subjects, not knowing about dejanews ? questions that show that
  the poster doesnt know how to search via dejanews / search engine ?

  My point is that people should be better educated about technology
  (which would require lessons in high-school, large improvements in
  user manuals, some improvements in user interfaces & bug amounts,
  and other well discussed subjects), as well as a change in the
  public's attitude - it should be clear that computers & software
  are not (at least not yet) intuitive, and some effort is needed
  to learn & use it. 

