x-mailer: Mozilla 3.01 (X11; I; SunOS 5.5.1 sun4m)


My feeling is that we definitely don't want (or need) compatible
kernels. We need to develop standardized API's for various uses,
but not a common kernel. There are so many ways to put together
an operating system. It can be monolithic or distributed, it can
be a microkernel design, it cen be Mach based or AT&T Unix based.
Should we abandon the possibility to use the design that fits the
job? No. So we don't want a common Unix kernel. The word Unix
has changed a lot. It used to mean "any operating system containing
bits of code from AT&T's Unix, even if it is completely incompatible".
Now, Unix means "Any operating system, regardless of origin, that
conforms to a specified set of requirements". Even MVS is Unix now.

The shell between the kernel and the application world should
be standardized. And this shell is the set of supported API's.
The Unix spec is a good start. It's by no means enough for all kinds
of applications, but it's enough for the important applications.
Database vendors will always have to tune for all possible
architectures, so don't use database engines to evaluate portability.
All vendors will provide special (sometimes proprietary) hooks
for database engines. As we (well, I, at least) want a multitude
of kernels, the kernels will have different kinds of hooks for
special software like this.

Most other kinds of software should be able to recompile
without problems. And this is where the Unix spec will have to
develop first. The standardized API's should be expanded to
cover everything we need for programs that don't need special hooks
into the OS for speed or functionality. The biggest headache just
now is the step to 64-bit architectures.

There should also be some common API for management. Different vendors
could still have different configuration files with different names
(perhaps no configuration files at all, just internal databases).
But there should be a common administration API. This API should
be open enough to be able to embrace routers, PC's, etc. For the
time beeing, look at Tivoli or CA Unicenter for this kind of
functionality. This is more complex than You would believe, as
a common administration interface for Unix must be flexible enough
to run on everything that is Unix compliant, including MVS
and other operating systems that have suddenly become 'Unix'.

To sum up: Yes, we want a better set of common API's, but No,
we don't want a common Unix kernel.

-- 
