Subject: Pentium processor voltages for system upgrade
Date: 1997/02/10
Message-ID: <5dnnq2$udo@decius.ultra.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 217841031
organization: UltraNet Communications, Inc.
newsgroups: comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.dec.micro


I have a DEC Celebris GL system which has a Socket 7 and a 30-pin
Voltage Regulator Module socket with a pass-thru shunt installed in
it.  The DEC documentation states that I have to install a voltage
regulator in the VRM socket to run a processor different than the
90MHz Pentium originally installed.

From Tom's very excellent hardware guide website
http://www.sysdoc.pair.com/

I figured out that the P90, marked SX968, operates at the STD voltage
range of 3.135V ~ 3.6V.  Some P133s and P150s operate at the same STD
voltage range, so I could simply drop one of these in after changing
jumpers on the Celebris motherboard.

Also that many P166 or P200 have the VRE voltage range of 3.4V ~ 3.6V.

My question is... Why would I need a voltage regulator for a P166 or
P200, when its operating voltage range is smaller than the STD
voltage?

Thanks much.  All these fine details about processor voltages for
processors with the same pinout is darn confusing.  I don't want to
fry a Pentium with too much voltage... Ben Myers





