Terry Kennedy wrote:
> 
> Boris Gjenero <bgjenero@undergrad.math.uwaterloo.ca> writes:
> > I concluded that the microcontroller on the drive was shutting it down
> > because it found a fault.  Is there any way to get error codes out of or
> > run detailed diagnostics on RD53 drives (I don't think the diagnostics
> > on the VAXstation are good enough--I need lower level diagnostics)?
> > Does anybody recognize this failure mode?
> 
>   This is the absolutely most common failure of RD53's (just like bad bear-
> ings are the most common fault on the RD54's).
> 
>   Someone who spent days on this (not me!) decided that the problem was
> excessive current draw by the spindle motor. I don't think they had a long-
> term fix, but I think they had some workaround to let the disk spin up long
> enough to be backed up.

Well, I had tried shorting the current sensing resistor (the one between
the common point on the windings and ground) after first making sure the
motor or its driving circuit wasn't about to melt if I did that.  It
didn't improve things one bit.  All it did was change the period of the
oscillations on the drive that kept turning the motor on and off at a
few Hz.
I don't know... maybe that made it think there wasn't enough current
draw, or maybe there is some other sensing as well.  Can you ask the
person who found a workaround what they did?  

> prompt, I forget which). If you want to define a new drive type to get the
> maximum capacity from your drive, you have to input some weird MSCP param-
> eters, the meaning of which is undocumented and obscure.

I see a list of parameters in the faq for the machine.  I understand
what they are, but I've never used such detailed parameters.  However,
any decent drive manufacturer should know them.  Of course, there are
non-decent drive manufacturers out there.  I'll just have to be careful.

