This whole thread has brought a feeling of nostalgia.

Back in my days at DEC, one of the internal notesfiles I participated in
was the MARKETING notesfile.  Virtually everything being said here was
said there, in similar words, in the mid to late '80's.  Plays on the
"Digital Has it Now" slogan were plentiful; a common one was "Digital
has it now; you can have it tomorrow."  (Well, maybe next year.)  Anyone
remember "Digital Changes the Way the World Works"?  (That one got
turned into "Digital Works the Way the World Changes - Slowly".)

Of course, the problem is that the MARKETING notesfile was created by an
engineer, and was frequented, for the most part, by engineers.  The real
marketing people hadn't the foggiest idea it was there - not that they
would have bothered to read it anyway.  There *was* participation from
people in the field - e.g., sales technical support, sometimes even real
salesmen - and they generally agreed with what the engineers were
saying.  (In fact, on several occasions, I found that engineering and
the people really dealing with customers agreed on issues that
"marketing" - management, in general - took a 180 degree different
course on, to the continuing consternation of those actually building
and selling things (and to the eventual serious decline in DEC's
fortunes).)

Unfortunately, despite all the pain and upheaval, effective marketing
remains a lesson DEC does not appear to have learned.  (They *did*
eventually learn of the need to advertise - but they seem to have gone
out of their way to hire *hot* agencies who would do trendy, flashy ads
that did nothing to build a good image, much less actually help sell
product.)

Sad.  Really sad.
							-- Jerry
					[Who is still holding on to some
					 DEC shares - bought "cheap" at
					 employee prices - in the hope
					 that it will some day be worth
					 what he paid for it....]



