Chris Ulrich wrote:
> 
>   My boss's boss has asked me to look into clusterig options
> for dec unix.

The place to start is http://www.unix.digital.com/cluster/index.html

>  He is interested in such things as dssi support,
> ci support, that new scsi bus sharing support, that sort of
> thing.

Digital UNIX does not support dssi or ci interconnects on Alpha systems.
Shared scsi and Memory Channel are the main interconnects.

>  My guess is that the goal is first failure tolerance
> and after that maybe performance.  They are planning on putting
> a database of some sort onto a couple of machines with one
> either as a load balancing machine or a hot spare.  My guess
> is that if they do this, it will have to come at the application
> level and not at the hardware / os level.  The proposed database
> software is oracle.

The Oracle parallel server option is supported over Memory Channel on
Digital UNIX. This provides enhanced performance as well as failover, so
it seems like a good place to start.
Digital calls this the "TruCluster Production Server" and has a fair
amount of information on http://www.unix.digital.com/cluster/index.html

You can find some less sales oriented stuff in the online Digital
Technical Journal at http://www.digital.com:80/info/DTJ/ and below,
especially "Design of the TruCluster Multicomputer System for the
Digital UNIX Environment" (http://www.digital.com:80/DTJL01/DTJL01.HTM)
and "Performance Measurement of TruCluster Systems under the TPC-C
Benchmark" (http://www.digital.com:80/DTJN04/DTJN04HM.HTM)

The Digital UNIX FAQ at
ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/dec-faq/Digital-UNIX might have some
information too, but I haven't looked at it lately.

-- 
