 > Afford, well, what's money.  I am buying around a terabyte of disk this
 > year and probably two TB next year.  At the moment only 200 gig is going
 > on an SGI (for a total of 300 gig) but I'm expecting to run out of that
 > sooner than anyone else thinks.  A TB one one machine is at least possible
 > sometime next year.  There are only six users on this system.

 > So anyway, it does happen.

Nobody has said that it doesn't happen.  If you're planning to put
more than one terabyte of disk in a single logical disk volume (i.e.,
xlv) running IRIX on one of the platforms which uses a 32-bit kernel,
then yes, you'll run into a limitation.  You could always split up
your disks into multiple logical disk volumes.  It shouldn't be too
much of a hardship to have to partition your disk farm into
terabyte-sized disk volumes.

If you're hosting those six users you mention on our server systems or
on our mid-range workstations (e.g., Indigo2 R10000), rather than our
lower-end workstation systems (e.g., Indy, the special Indy-derived
Challenge S, Indigo2's with R4xxx CPU's, etc.), they'll run the fully
64-bit versions of IRIX which Martin Knoblauch has said will support 1
million terabytes per logical disk volume, so you could have your
entire disk farm be a single logical disk volume if you really want.

I certainly hope for your users' sake that you aren't hosting them on
our low-end systems if they are running applications that crunch
terabytes of disk space.  If you can afford this kind of disk space,
you can afford to provide them with a system with more scalable CPU
and I/O oomph, methinks.

