newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
x-admin: news@aol.com


I know nothing about marketing.  I also have heard that the less you know
about a subject, the easier it seems.  Despite this situation I will
suggest a marketing campaign for DEC.  Disclaimer: I am not a marketer but
I play one on TV.

Just say "we have 500 mghtz!"!!". . . people like big numbers.  Digital
hints at this with  the "fastest microprocessers on the planet" sentence
they stick on the end of their print adds . . . but it is not the main
theme.   Their TV adds seem really lame (IMO) with some kind of vague
message that Digital systems can be upgraded . . . if I did not know
better I would think Digital is trying to give a reason for buying low
performance hardware ("o.k. we are not the fastest, but our computers will
give you years of service").

I am amazed how many people are excited about the 533 exponential powerpc,
which will not be available till **JUNE**.  DEC has 500 boxes right now,
and and they have dropped prices to much lower levels.  The media had many
articles on the exponential 533 (Byte, Wall Street Journal) I have never
seen *any* about the 500 Alpha box (500 alpha is also faster at same clock
speed).

Car magazines typically use 1-2 monthly issues to feature a very high
performance car, even if few readers can afford it.  Why can't BYTE or PC
do any article on a 500 mghtz ALPHA box with NT?  What about a test drive
of the 770 mghtz box DEC demo'd at comdex . . . not in production yet, but
outrageous.

The scientific/engineering market seems like a natural market for Digital,
as they have very good fp performance.  I know several people in academia
who run pentium pros.  The ALPHA has a big lead and this lead can only get
much larger (the 21264 will be much faster, Intel does not have a real
speed increase until ~2 years from now.  If you do not believe look at
Intel news group)

When the 21264 and 633 speed bump come along, it would be a shame to keep
these features secret.  

my 0.02 dollars worth . . . later . . .

Andrew



