> 
> >Even those lines are not that clear. Take any SGI workstation, except the
> >R10k Indigo^2 and the Octane, running IRIX 6.x. You have 32bit addresses (ie
> >32bit pointers) but you can use all the instructions and regusters of the
> >64bit ABI. Is that a 32bit OS .. I would tend to think it is 64bit.
> 
> Are you willing to give Sun credit for our Ultra running a 64-bit OS
> on this basis?  We have 64 bit registers, too.
> (...)

Pardon? Correct me if I'm wrong, but I think currently (Release <=
2.5.1) you can't even use 64 bit in user land without pain, because
register contents might be clobbered across function calls due to
possible register fill/spill (no space in the stack frame in the
current ABI).

That's rather an ABI issue than an OS issue, I concede.

I don't care at all about Terabytes of address space (I won't need
them until 2020 or so, with my user profile), but not to be able to
do 64bit arithmetic without pain while the CPU supports it, is frus-
trating.
