I help run a web site running on a DEC Alpha.  We had two SCSI disks: an
RZ26 as the primary and a Seagate 2G disk.  The root filesystem was UFS
on the RZ26 with the rest of the disk and all of the Seagate being under
the control of LSM.  The /usr filesystem and all user home directories were
on advfs filesystems on the LSM managed areas.

Yesterday the Seagate died.  Since we didn't really have a need for the
full 2G disk, we replaced it with a 1G Seagate.  The problem is, we can't
initialize it.  We can get the system to come up in single-user mode with
only the root filesystem mounted.  I remount the filesystem for rw access,
but whenever a process tries to write to the disk (disklabel or anything
else) the system panics and drops down to the boot prompt.

To furthur complicate matters, the system owner has managed to lose the
installation CD.  He is off looking for it, but does anybody have any 
suggestions as to what I can do in the meantime to get the filesystems
recreated so that I can restore from my backup tapes?

Thanks.
	/dwight

