In the CMOS setup there is a group called Power. In this section you can
set the different settings for Power Management. The settings are for
Standby (set for power down from 1-30 minutes) and Supend (from 1 hr. to
3 hrs.). In DOS and Windows you had both the Standby and Supend. When
Win95 came out, the terms were changed. Now there is no Standby (the
less than 1 hr settings), instead you have Monitor Settings that provide
about the same function. The settings are still in the CMOS in case you
run DOS/WIN, but are not used by Win95.

If you're going into a power saving mode in about 10 minutes, then you
really are going into a monitor power down (the CPU continues to run).
You "wake" the system by moving the mouse or typing a key. This turns on
the power to the monitor. Sometimes the monitor does not "wake" up and
this could be related to a problem with the monitor. 

Try changing the different settings in the display properties\screen
save settings and see if that makes a difference.

You can also disable the CMOS Power Management (set to disable) and the
monitor standby mode will still operate. Try that and see if the monitor
wakes up.

