newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


Our 'old' environment is a VAX 6000-series machine, which has a DMB-32
parallel interface on the back, hooked to an LP29 printer.

This has worked great for years, and our operators have enjoyed the ability
to greatly control the printer (set job back N pages, etc).

We're now migrating to an ALphaserver 4100, and no such parallel interface
is available.    Our only solution to hook up the LP29 parallel printer
is via a network print box; in this case a rapidprint 200.   (I guess
similar to a DECserver 250, a netprint box, etc).

Although the printing with the new environment "works", the main problem
we have is the loss over job control.    The print box essentially sucks
all (or large parts) of the job from the system at a time, thus giving my
operators little or no control over print jobs after they've been submitted.
(for example, we must have the ability to step the jobs back N pages
for re-alignment, or re-queue the job altogether from the start).   

In the old environment, the vax more or less "controlled" the printer.
Now, jobs get buffered into the network print box, and no longer have
that control.

Does anyone out there have suggestions for me?   Anything I can do to
perhapsd 'not' buffer jobs so heavily into the rapidprint-200, or
could there perhaps be some other alternative?

As I said from the beginning, it WORKS, but we've lost 'control'.

Thanks for any help you can give on this.   ("new technology
isn't ALWAYS better")


