Michael Engel <engel@numerik.fb6.uni-siegen.de> writes:
> So we came up with the idea of writing a new firmware for the 200/MC. This 
> shouldn't be _too_ hard, considering most of the IC's in the DECserver are
> pretty standard (68000 CPU, 2681 UARTs, LANCE AMD 7990 ethernet, 2816 EEPROM), 
> there is some free source code available for small TCP/IP implementations 
> and the 200/MC has 384 kB RAM, which should be sufficient for a tiny TCP/IP
> implementation.

  I don't think it's worth your time to do this - if you *really* want to do
it, get a used DECserver 500 or 550 which has a nice PDP-11 in it and standard
I/O cards. You can add a disk controller and drive and boot 2.11BSD on it 8-)

> If I understand correctly, the 200/MC boots its firmware via MOP (for which
> a free implementation is available). So, there would be an easy way to 
> download new firmware. 

  Are you referring to my RSTS/E MOP server, or is there another package out
there? If it's another one, please send me a pointer so I can update my ar-
chives.

> - What binary format does the 200/MC expect to receive ? Plain binary, some
>   kind of S-record format or s.th. completely different ?

  It's a 68K binary with an RSX-11 task header on the front. As I recall,
this is documented in the MOP spec.

> - What is the memory map of the 200/MC, i.e. at which addresses can I find 
>   the various devices (UART's, LANCE ethernet, 2816 EEPROM etc.) ?
> - Which device produces which M68000 interrupt ? Are the interrupts auto-
>   vectored or not ?
> - What function does the PGA chip on the DECserver provide ?
>
> If anyone has any hints or can lead me to some documentation available from
> digital, I'd be very happy !

  You want EK-DSRVB-TM, which gives all of the above information as well as
the layout of the configuration EEPROM. Unfortunately, that manual isn't
available. It should still be part of the MDS11 microfiche library, but that
is a several thousand dollar item. You can get EK-DSRVA-TM, which should be
the equivalent manual for the DS100. That might be enough to get you started
(and see below for more info).

  That probably isn't enough to help you - the EPROM on the DS200 contains
a tiny operating system as well as the boot loader, and the DS200 code uses
some of the routines in that EPROM once the booted code is running. I don't
think the details of the EPROM's entry points were ever documented.

  There's a resident debugger in the DS200 load image. To access it, you need
to be connected to port 1. Do the following:

Local> set priv
Local> set port 1 odt enabled
Local> odt

  Note that if you're running a recent DS200 load image, the server will
crash with a watchdog timer panic.

  Oh, and of course you need to have already booted the DECserver from some-
where. You don't need a LAT host for this, though, so it might help you in
poking around the server's innards.

