In article <5d9beq$lo8@news.usaor.net>, nero@nero.in-design.com (Tamer Ziady) writes:
>
>I have found me an OLD Dec 350 PRO, and was wondering what can this
 machine do I am wondering about it's speed, and what OS it runs, 

Including can one get hardware for it these days, and if yes from where. 
 Also what OS can I run on it besides ULTRIX (which I cannot afford) or VMs.
>

The PRO-350 is a PDP-11 in a small box.  It runs P/OS, which is a modified
version of RSX-11M with some M-Plus stuff.  I've heard of versions of RT-11
that ran on it, and one or more Unix clones, but I've never run either of
them myself.

I regret to say that they have very little value these days: I haven't
even turned the power on on mine for several years.  Used parts ought
to be very cheap at any of the regular DEC equipment dealers, and I've
heard of people giving away whole systems just to avoid throwing them
in the trash.  There are various "for sale or trade" newsgroups that
you could watch for this.

Compared with other desktop systems that were made at the same time as
the PRO-350, it's fairly fast.  It's archetecture allowed it to address
much more memory more effectively than the 8088 based IBM-PC and clones
that were it's main competition at the time, and it runs a truly multi-
tasking (and multi-user, though that's tricky on a PRO) operating system.

Compared with the cheapest PCs you can get now, it's rather slow: but
that's true when you compare any 15 year old computer with a new one.

It does not run Ultrix or VMS, those are for the VAX and Alpha archetectures.

