On 5 Feb 1997, Curt Smith wrote:
> I presume you are saying that >>2/4Gb file systems and files?  What other
> OS internals are affected by 64 bits?  time_t? 

  The term itself is rather vague.  If you could pick a single key factor, I
would say, does said OS use 64-bit memory addressing?  The 64-bit Alpha uses
that, so does the OS take advantage of it?  There are other things to be said
for 64-bit math, but that can usually be emulated (at a very large performance
loss).  You can't emulate memory addressing.

  The size of filesystems and such isn't dependent on the OS being 64-bit as
much as you'd think.  Obviously, if you're storing a file size, then you need
more then 32-bits to get more then 4 gigs.  But that can be emulated (I have
heard of methods that store files bigger then 4 gig in 4 gig "chunks" when you
look at the internals, or just do a 64-bit int in software).  But most *disks*
address by blocks or sectors, and not by individual bytes.

> I also presume that you are NOT saying that gcc supports long == 64 bits on 
> 64 bit HW.

  That is (part of) what it means on all the Alpha's we have here.

