Ron Natalie <ron@sensor.com> writes:

> > Make sure that your article details just which operating systems have the
> > right  to use the name "Unix".  There's a reason that many manufacturers don't
> > call their operating systems "Unix", but rather something close.
> 
> Actually, it's not because they don't have the right.  Most chose
> to use their own marketing terms.  HP/UX, AIX, Solaris, IRIX, all
> could have used UNIX.

Only if they want to be sued. At least some of those systems are not
even close to meeting the specification.

> It's the UNIX clones (LINUX, NetBSD, Coherent, etc...) that have
> problems.
> 
> I don't think there are any UNIX's in commercial use that derive
> their lineage from the Western Electric roots that don't also
> meet the other requirements for the trademark.

To use the UNIX trademark, a system must meet the definition of UNIX
as defined by X/Open, the owner of the trademark. (They got it from
SCO who bought USL from Novell who bought it from AT&T.) 

Most OSes thought of as UNIX don't meet these standards (which are
MUCH more rigorous than Posix) and at least one which does is NOT
derived from the old AT&T code at all. It comes from CMU Mach. (It's
also 64 bit and IS call UNIX by its owner, Digital.)
