In <5dat5j$50@portal.gmu.edu>, rraffer1@osf1.gmu.edu (Ryan M Rafferty) writes:
>My question is, how much did the first IBM PC's cost when they were first
>released?  What was the stock amount of memory, and did they come with a
>display adapter?
>
>Thanks,
>Ryan Rafferty
>
>--
>

There were two configurations announced on August 12, 1981.
Configuration 1 was a type 5150-813, while configuration 2 was a
type 5150-824.  Configuration 1:  Includes the System Unit/Keyboard,
40KB of ROM, 48KB of user memory, programmable speaker, cassette
attachment jack, 5 1/4 Inch Diskette Drive Adapter, one 5 1/4
Inch-160KB Diskette Drive and an Asynchronous Communications
Adapter.  Configuration 2:  Same as Configuration 1 but with 64KB of
user memory and two 5 1/4 Inch-160KB Diskette Drives.

Each configuration uses two of the five system expansion slots in
the System Unit, one for the 5 1/4 Inch Diskette Drive Adapter and
one for the Asynchronous Communications Adapter.  One of the three
remaining system expansion slots must be used for an adapter to
support a video output device.  The customer must order and install
an adapter and either a display, monitor, or TV as follows: The
IBM Monochrome Display requires an IBM Monochrome Display and
Printer Adapter.  A customer-supplied color or black and white
monitor or TV requires the IBM Color/Graphics Monitor Adapter.  If a
TV is used, the customer must also provide an RF modulator.  The two
remaining system expansion slots may be used to support
customer-installable options:  The IBM 80 CPS Matrix Printer
requires either the IBM Monochrome Display and Printer Adapter
(which may already be installed to support the IBM Monochrome
Display) or the IBM Printer Adapter.  32KB or 64KB memory
expansions (if installed on a Configuration 1, a 16KB memory
expansion, which does not require a system expansion slot, is
required to fully populate the system expansion board to 64KB).
Note:  The maximum memory which may be installed on a standard
configuration after a video adapter option is installed is 192KB
(64KB on the system expansion board and two 64KB Memory Expansion
Options in the system expansion slots).  The Game Control Adapter
supports customer-supplied paddles or joysticks for video game
interaction.  A second 5 1/4 Inch-160KB Diskette Drive may be
installed on Configuration 1 and will utilize the already installed
5 1/4 Inch Diskette Drive Adapter.

According to my information, the announced prices of the systems
were (US)$2,385 for Configuration 1, and $3,045 for Configuration 2.
The mono-display (Type 5151-001) was $345, and the matrix printer
(Type 5152) was $755.  The mono-display/printer adapter was $335,
while the CGA was $300.

Dave

P.S. Standard Disclaimer: I speak only for myself, not for my
company.

P.P.S.  Oh, DOS was announced for $40, and required 12K of memory
for itself.




