Did they ever have it?  I just picked up two VAXstation 2000 machines at
the university surplus sale ($10.80 canadian, but no monitors and only
one has the keyboard and that big cable).  After that, I've been going
through info on the net on those things to learn more about them.
I was hoping to play with and learn about VMS on these.  However, from
what I've read, Digital would want something like $300 to just
*transfer* a VMS license.  No way...  if I get these to boot they'll be
running something else, probably FreeBSD.
Also, it seems some of the diagnostis already on the machine actually
require a license.  Wow!  I've never seen such fascist licensing
restrictions, except possibly the 10 connection limit on Windows NT
workstation.
Of course, these license restrictions are all legal.  However, they are
the things that would make many people turn away if they read the fine
print.

Few individuals have a personal (non-business) need for VMS or
VAXstations, but I'm sure there are enough people who would like to have
them.  Making an operating system easily available for personal use may
have a big effect on future business use as well, as people who have
used it before and liked it want to use it again.  Is VMS going to last
if there are no new users except for those joining compaines that have
used it for a long time?  Think about Unix, and how there is Linux,
FreeBSD and FreeSCO.  There are also commercial unices that are a couple
hundred dollars or less.
Heck, Digital's stuff doesn't even appear available to individuals.  How
can they expect it to sell?

After reading all the positive stuff about Digital in the jargon file, I
sure didn't expect this.

BTW.  Does anybody know the specs for the video from that BNC connector
on the VS2000.

