> What always struck me as amazing, was the fact that in plain old DOS,
> you had pipes and globbing, and all them snazzy features of real
> shells.  Except globbing was brokenly implemented (each command had to
> do its own globbing, some did, some didn't), and the only command (to my

Well, in a few cases it can be an advantage not to have the shell do
globbing.  Sometimes when globbing is done by the shell, the results may
not not be as nice as you expected.  
Some C compilers had libraries that you could link with so that it'd
seem to main as if the shell did the globbing.

> knowledge) that understood pipes were the ``more'' command, and it
> _only_ understood pipes (i.e. no ``more foo.bar'').

Well, first of all, they weren't really pipes.  The output of one
command goes into a file and the second command reads from the file. 
However, it was good enough for many applications, and a reasonable
implementation for a single tasking system.  
Any command that used the DOS console I/O functions could use pipes (as
well as redirection).  This was not limited to more. 

The thing that now *really* amazes me with MS-DOS is that it wasn't too
buggy.  It rarely crashed just due to itself.  That's odd for something
made by Microsoft.

BTW.  It's interesting how much of DOS is just like CP/M or UNIX (well,
cheap inferior implementations of UNIX functionality).  It's not exactly
Bill's idea. 

