I replaced the processor of this machine (i486DX2/66) with a Kingston I
replaced the processor of this machine (i486DX2/66) with a Kingston
TurboChip 133 (AM5x86-P75, clock quadrupled 486). It wasn't necessary to
change any BIOS settings or jumpers in the motherboard. The machine
booted without problems.

However I didn't notice any improvements in my regular use. Intrigued, I
used Wintune 95 with both processors with the folowing results:

                MIPS  MFLOPS
i486DX2/66       53     14
TurboChip 133    38     18

Besides the small improvement in the FPU benchmark, only the RAM read
test improved (it "measures" cache efficiency, which is greater in the
AM5x86). All the other benchmarks showed worst values.

I also tried the diagnostics program QAPlus 4.77 which came with the
machine and it showed that the performance with the TurboChip was
somewhere between a 486DX and a 486DX2.

I'm astonished with the performance decrease, especially in the integer
performance. Does anybody have an idea about what is going on? Are there
any suggestions?

Some information about the DECpc

DECpc LPx 466d2
Sound Blaster 16 MCD + Mitsumi FX001D CD-ROM
PhoenixBIOS A486 Version 1.01.02 (2/28/1993)
Windows 95 + Service Pack 1 + Kernel, OLE32 and password list "advanced"
updates
The ROM BIOS doesn't have any settings related with CPU types
The motherboard doesn't have any jumpers related with CPU types

