In general there shouldn't be much more to it than run
WINNT32 /B from the Windows NT 4.0 CD, but...

The "proper" way:

1) Do a complete backup of your system
2) Update the Emergency Repair Diskette
3) Make sure you can restore the backup (to another disk, on another
   system ;-)
4) Upgrade the BIOS to the latest release (whatever it is), and
   make sure the system still works
5) Boot Windows NT 3.51 in VGA mode
6) Stop all services
7) Run WINNT32 /B from the Windows NT 4.0 CD-ROM and when asked
   tell this is an upgrade
8) See that you can boot OK
9) Install any new operating system options you may want to use
   (one approach is to install everything "just in case" so that
   a service pak installation updates also those)
10) Install any new[er] drivers (video, sound, network)
11) Install Service Pak 2 + the post-SP2 kernel-fix (or if you
    want to be on the safer side, install only SP1)
12) Make sure what you've got still works as intended

Note! Implicit in above steps is: Read installation guides and
release notes ("readme" files) relevant to the step before
actually doing anything + follow the guides ;-)

In practice doing steps from number 7 and onwards is enough
(and usually by skipping the installation guides :-), but it 
is up to you if you want to be safe or possibly sorry later... ;-)

regards,
...petri.backstrom@icl.fi
   ICL Data Oy
   Finland

P.S. I've had no problems upgrading Celebris XL systems to 
     Windows NT 4.0 (different CPUs, single & dual, different
     video adapters, network adapters, etc.).

