> "Glen H. Mullen" <gmullen@ghg.net> wrote:
>
> AFAIK you have to use a "redirector" that translates the clustername
> to one of the available node's real name. The filesystem layer has
> such a redirector, but I doubt that SQLNET has one yet.  Check with
> Oracle for this.
>
> Anders
>
> >We have just setup Oracle 7.3 WGS on a DEC NT Cluster I managed to get
> >the failover between the servers working by writing a script to start
> >the DB the Oracle Failover Client never would work.  The problem we have
> >not been able to resolve to resolve is connecting to the cluster through
> >SQL*Net.  Does anyone have experience getting SQL*Net 2.3 to talk to an
> >Oracle 7.3 WGS using the cluster name.  I have tried named pipes and
> >TCP/IP but they can only connect using the host name of the individual
> >server.  I would appreicate any ideas or a confirmation that this will
> >not work.
>
> >Thanks
> >Glen Mullen
> >gmullen@ghg.net
>
> Anders �stling - anders@liga.se

OPS actually looks as separate instances. There is two ways
to make it look as single instance.

First - to add all hosts in address list in tnsnames.ora entry.
To balance load of nodes, you must specify different order of
nodes in different clients.

Second - to use feature of TNS listener, which can deal with
dispatchers from different hosts. All you need is
specify MTS_SERVICE_ADDRESS to point to listener on given host.
Client sends request to listener, which transparently redirects
request to least used dispatcher on any of specified node.
To balanse load of nodes, you can change numbers of dispatchers on
given node.

Note, that single listener is a point of failure. On our HP-UX cluster,
we use "floating" IP address feature, that gives ability to
"floating" of listener with IP address from failed node to another.
I don't have any information about analogs on DEC clusters.

