  I've been an OS/2 bigot for some considerable time now, in relative
terms, and continue to do serious development on that particular
computer. It's an "aging" 486DX2-66 with 24 megs of RAM and just under a
gig of hard drive space, and what impresses me most with it isn't the PM
(it has its uses) but the fact that so much GNU stuff has been ported to
it that it's a pleasure to use some actual command-line utilities that
leave Windows 95's many cut-and-paste "shortcuts" in the dust.

  The Windows 95 machine is a P133 with just over a gig of HD and 16
megs of RAM. Granted, that might not be a lot of RAM, but it still seems
that for many things, the 486 running OS/2 simply leaves Windows 95
begging in terms of performance.

  That said, while the Windows 95 interface is very nice if you don't
know what you're doing, it would still be quite beyond today's newbies
even if it were by some feat of software engineering made to be totally
psychic. There are simply too few people relative to the general
computing public who understand the concept of a stream or of a block
device to even begin to "intuit" his way under the hood, and too many
people who've been mislead by popular media into believing that we have
entered the Age of Pushbutton Information.

  Now, how the hell DO I get this thing to change fonts?

  Celebrate your "dead" operating system. It'll be some time before the
claims of performance by yesterday's competition's sales hypes can
deliver in some future version of NT.

