|   What always struck me as amazing, was the fact that in plain old DOS,
|   you had pipes and globbing, and all them snazzy features of real
|   shells.  Except globbing was brokenly implemented (each command had to
|   do its own globbing, some did, some didn't), and the only command (to my
|   knowledge) that understood pipes were the ``more'' command, and it
|   _only_ understood pipes (i.e. no ``more foo.bar'').

In a non-multitasking environment such as MSDOS, pipes are pretty
broken too.

f1 | f2

runs f1, writing the output to a temporary file, then, once f1 has
finished starts f2, redirecting its standard input from that file. Do
a:

DIR | MORE

and spot the two extra files in the listing!

Of course, if f1 never terminates, there will never be any output from
the pipe. This is pretty silly really.

Globbing is very broken in DOS. For that there is _no_ good excuse.

Bob
