Glen H. Mullen wrote:
> 
> We have just setup Oracle 7.3 WGS on a DEC NT Cluster I managed to get
> the failover between the servers working by writing a script to start
> the DB the Oracle Failover Client never would work.  The problem we have
> not been able to resolve to resolve is connecting to the cluster through
> SQL*Net.  Does anyone have experience getting SQL*Net 2.3 to talk to an
> Oracle 7.3 WGS using the cluster name.  I have tried named pipes and
> TCP/IP but they can only connect using the host name of the individual
> server.  I would appreicate any ideas or a confirmation that this will
> not work.

From: 	Jack McCrorey
Sent: 	Wednesday, February 05, 1997 4:02 PM
To: 	'gmullen@ghg.net'
Subject: 	Digital Clusters and Oracle 7.3

Hello Glen,
 I am responding to your question about Digital Clusters and Oracle 7.3.
Oracle 7.3 will be supported with Digital Clusters 1.1 for Windows NT
4.0
and Service Pack 2 for Digital Clusters 1.0 for Windows NT 3.51. These
releases are in field test now and should be available in March.
 IP failover will only be supported on Windows NT 4.0.

 Regarding your current problem ... Oracle 7.3 and Clusters 1.0 will not
work together. The problem with Named pipes is that you are using a 
Script rather than the Oracle failover object to start the DB. The
Oracle
failover object is the one who sets up the pipe for the Listener on the
Server. By using the script, the pipe is not identified properly to the
cluster
software and the clients will not be able to resolve the pipe name.
Actually,
it's the combination of Cluster alias and pipe name that can not get
resolved.
Unfortunately, you can not use the Oracle failover object to set up the
named 
pipe because it doesn't work with Oracle 7.3.  I do not know of a work
around 
until the new Cluster software is available.

-- 
Carl Appellof
not speaking for
Digital Equipment Corporation



