geest3+@pitt.edu (Gregg E Economou):
> somewhere, but- forget about any kind of devices that use rts/cts signalling. 
> the ports on the 3100 have no modem control capabilities, so theyll work 
> fine on a dumb terminal but theywont work on a modem, sorta sux. 

They have some limited modem control support (dtr and dcd) or flow control
by dsr/dtr, but not all at the same time.  Connecting a modem is not a
problem but it is limited to 9600 bps and forget about compression.
But then remember that the machine is the 1989 generation and try to
think back what kind of modems had been available at that time.
The sad part of the story is that the chips inside are capable of real
modem control and even 19200 bps but that's not supported.
The already mentioned DS3100 documentation file may have some data
about this so if you feel like it you may be able to solder a modern
plug to a chip and get the flow control.  Having a driver to support
this is another issue, though (maybe NetBSD ?).

Greetings,
				Ripley
