On 3 Feb 1997, Old-Fashioned Staffordshire Plate... wrote:


I'm going to disagree slightly with Tom here.

...
> This was when risc systems running unix became
> available from several vendors and where cheaper/faster than vaxes, so a bunch
> of meeting going trade rag reading managers jumped ship and used the 'open'
> buzzword to justify and ass-cover the move.

Hum. First creak was that the unis went to 780s and BSD for teaching. Note,
that many of them used VMS for internal admin and library systems. The big
`RISC rush', was to Suns that where MUCH better bang for buck than Vaxen,
be they BSD of VMS shod. MIPS, Pyramid, Convex and an unsung cast of
many also did there bit. But, by then the unis had plenty of unix savy
grads to keep the bixes alive.

At this time, DOS was the biz buzz word... Along with the PC. Vaxen
DID make large inraods as well in the biz area.

> The people who make purchasing decisions, and the trade rags that sell them
> to the vendors also don't know or care about this either. It's not a factor in
> 'the marketplace' where the bill gates snake oil salesmen of the world thrive.
> They buy the bgNT CD and throw it on the tech. person's desk."Here, learn this.
> Make it work. It's the new corporate standard. I'm going with the bg salesman
> to lunch". 

Well, the odd thing is, that one of the bigger players in the biz area is...
Sun. LOTS of Sun boxes are going in to run Orrible or Ingrid. Lots
of HP and SGIs are as well. NT is picking up a bit from this, but the
BIG pro NT push is from DOS via Lozedoze 3.x

Te irony is, many campuses are now embacing the M$ crap as `the standard'
to inflict on their unknowing and inocent prey... Yet, this should be
the most demanding and savy market of all. So, where did DEC stuff it?

First BIG error was killing VMS on campus. First VMS was VERY big $$$,
vs $200 for BSD. No brainer for the cash strapped. Then came the
Jupiter fiasco, and the `migration' to VMS. Many of the sites that
may have till then coughed the ante for VMS, then _ran_ way from VMS and
anything DEC.

By the time DEC woke up, and brought in CSLG, it was to a large degree
too late. The little VMS expertese on campus had gone from most places.
Those left now knew Unix, mostly BDS or SunOS, or DOS. Lots of PC
hit desk in those days, and a rag tag of DOS fixers had to be installed
as well ;| These DOS and Unix people are now the ones who are now
pushing unix and M$. To most of them, VMS *IS* a dirty word; it is the
780 or 750 that was as slow as bog compared to the Sun. The fact
that there was 1 user on the Sun, ( or the PC in that case ), and 50
or a hundred on the vax has near 0 effect on their impression. And these
impressions are still killing VMS today.

Curtin is a fair example. CS and EE are pretty self contained and
unix based. Alphas with OSF are popular there. VMS on an alpha is
met with a deathly silence. DOS/Windoze is scorned there, but guess
how most get to the unices! The admin and Library and mail runs on
the cluster. Still know as `The Vax' or the like, though there is
now only 1 Vax left, and most can not log in to it... PMDF dropped
in a heap last night, so I'm expecting a slew of `why don't we run
mail on a fast Pentium with NT...' type noises. This is instead
of an 8400 5/300 with 1 GB, and the cluster at 2 sites for disaster
tolerance. Near all disks are shadowed across the sites as well. There
is NO WAY NT or unix can do this. But, to the 5K or grads that
headed out the door a efw months ago, computing is losedoze. VMS
is that thing that screws your mail. ( once or twice a year I may add.
It's not as if it happens very often. )

So between the poeple you can hire, and the marketing hype, is it
any wonder so many semi-clueless managers are going unix or M$?

Assetion: Until DEC get a significant number of VMS fluent or at
least familiar grads homing out the doors of the colleges and unis,
they are just letting the slope get steeper and steeper for getting
or _keeping_ vms. Even if you know vms is better, if you can't get the
people to run it, you are dead in the water. So you HAVE to go unix
or NT `cause that all the people you can get know. And because you
need more support people per seat for unix or M$, they are very
quickly in the majority.

~Paul




