> right controller for the 81's.  Is it too much to hope for that I can 
> simply move the RA81's to the MVII and have the whole mess boot ??

  Probably won't work. I ran 4.2BSD on a 750 and I don't recall there being
any MicroVAX II support. In fact, I do recall that in one of the later 4.3
releases (4.3 Reno or Tahoe, or the internal release of a 4.4-beta with mixed
4.3 and 4.4 utilities that Ben Cohen and I did which we called "Carson City"
8-) there were comments about how you could almost get it to work on a Micro-
VAX II "if only...".

> Any suggestions besides the obvious of getting the 750 fixed... ?

  I had a VAX 11/785 running the aforementioned "Carson City" release and
suffered 3 simultaneous RA81 failures (out of 8 on that system). I decided
that rather than trying to resurrect that configuration, I'd move our pro-
ject over to a PC running an early (0.2.x) beta release of BSDI's BSD/OS
software. This PC was a 386DX/25 with 16MB of RAM and a pair of 500MB IDE
hard disks. The users were *amazed* at the performance increase this ob-
solete PC gave them.

  So, if you have sources to your project, I'd suggest a move to a Pentium
or even a 486-based PC running one of the 4BSD-derived systems. I'm partial
to BSDI, but any of the free BSD's should be fine for you as well. If your
project doesn't depend much on 4BSD-style internals, you could also try
Linux.

