Yes it was very good.

>It had a filesystem tree, like Unix,
No, UNIX was like PR1MOS. Both PR1MOS and UNIX were like Multics (see
ALT.OS.MULTICS). There is nothing new since Multics except the Graphical
User Interface (GUI).

>except that there were multiple roots and the directory separator was a ">"
Which is what Multics did.  It was a direction pointer.  So, you could 
go up a level by typing "<" rather than "..".

>The only thing worse was the absolutely BEWILDERING array of options to
>the compilers and linkers; most having to do with which type of
>executable you were making.
That's the point.  There were an absolutely bewildering array of target
codes that had to be produced.

>And you felt the "Fortranness" of the operating system all the time.
The command language was the best in the industry.  I coded in most of
them. For example, you could code a CASE statement in it.  UNIX also did this
well (of course it has an absolutely bewildering array of command languages).

>The machine came out when everyone and their brother was coming out
>with superminicomputers (DEC w/VAX, PR1ME, DG w/Eclipse).  We know who
>won that battle...
Yeh, the people who learned their craft at Prime, and all the others

>Whatever happened to the company?  I remember seeing their building just
>outside of Boston ten or fifteen years ago.
It was torpedoed.  Someone tried a (very) hostile take over.  Management
had to spend ALL of its time fighting that just as the industry was
changing, and couldn't spend it's time dealing with the change.  So, a
whole lot of people didn't have a chance to do something neat.

