Subject: Re: QUESTION: DEC SCSI drive, Windows96 and Sync data Transfer.
Date: 1997/02/02
Message-ID: <E4zv5o.A26@mv.mv.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 213978642
x-nntp-posting-host: gce.com
sender: everhart@arisia.gce.mv.com   (Glenn C. Everhart)
references: <32F4EFDC.6731@ix.netcom.com>
organization: General Cybernetic Engineering
reply-to: Everhart@GCE.Com
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



Sync data transfer in SCSI means it can send several data bytes in a burst
instead of one only before it needs an ack from the other end. VMS supports
this...can't say about other OSs.

Most newer SCSI disks support this too. However be aware...some of the older
port drivers (notably in older VAX 3100 models) cannot do synchronous and
so run with the slower asynch SCSI. All the Alpha drivers can handle sync.
Glenn Everhart



